INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4983,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10774243','Women and Authorship in Medical Toxicology: Canaries in the Coal Mine.','Neumann NR, Beauchamp GA.','2024','writ flourishing stagnation brevity portend boarded gettel gettel intensifying rearing hovering beneath canaries stagnation writ jmt commend jmt jmt jmt mycyk filardo graca sass i847 i847 abdalla abdalla orlas kelleher e2323872 lall zeidan s67 s74 gettel trockel mungo schmitgen storz akhavan yagnik neilan e13508 depress templeton sukhera nora 201905a linzer elon sogimcss seabury marriages m17 sweetman ferrier chepa lachish svirko alanazy alruwaili healthcare11152220','What if gender disparities in authorship reflect not implicit bias, but larger trends in emergency medicine and medical toxicology writ large? Gender bias has long been considered a driving force behind the differences in medical scholarly output between women and men [ 1 , 2 ]. As women in medical toxicology, we were therefore very interested in the excellent exploration by Love et al. of the relationship between gender diversity in medical toxicology and female authorship position [ 3 ]. There is optimistic evidence from the study by Love et al. to suggest that continued growth in female representation in medical toxicology is being reflected in an increasing rate of female first authorship. These findings suggest flourishing professional success early in women’s careers as larger numbers of women enter the field. However, the study also finds that these gains might not persist in mid- to late-career: the authors note a persistent lack of senior female authorship, females as expert authors, and women with NIH-supported research funding. Perhaps this disparity results from the fact that women, until more recently, have comprised a minority of medical toxicology trainees and thus may be reflective of an “experience-gap” between women and men. Alternatively, the potential for stagnation over time in female senior authorship may reflect a distinct phenomenon. We propose that it is worth considering whether gender differences in academic output and promotion status may not be reflective simply of prejudice (although this may play a part), but of larger trends in medicine affecting career longevity to which women may respond more significantly. The findings of Love et al. that women are underrepresented as senior authors, compounded by what is known about the relative brevity of women’s careers as emergency medicine physicians, may therefore portend that the rising cohort of female first authors are at risk of careers that are too abbreviated to support the transition to the senior author and mentor role. Medical toxicology in North America is most closely tied to emergency medicine, the majority of medical toxicologists being trained and double-boarded in both specialties [ 4 ]. Assuming that most publishing medical toxicologists practice in academics and emergency medicine, the persistent gap between men and women serving in senior author roles may reflect the known phenomenon that women leave academic medicine, academic emergency medicine, and general emergency medicine at younger ages than men [ 5 – 7 ]. A recent study by Gettel et al. quantified this disparity [ 7 ]. They found that the median career length of a woman emergency medicine physician is 10.5 years versus 17.5 for men [ 7 ]. Significantly, however, Gettel et al. found that while the median age of attrition from clinical practice was 12 years younger for women than men, age of attrition decreased significantly for both men and women during the years studied: men leaving approximately 6 years earlier at 53.5 in 2019 versus 59.2 in 2013, and women leaving at 43.7 in 2019 versus 45.6 in 2013. Such early retirement or reduction in work hours is in keeping with intensifying burnout and dissatisfaction with the field [ 7 – 9 ]. However, if all emergency medicine physicians are withdrawing earlier from practice, why might women leave before men? Chen et al. interestingly found that women, even after their child-rearing years, tend to leave academic medicine at higher rates than men, indicating factors beyond frequently cited stressors related to the caregiver role [ 5 ]. Of the work-related stressors contributing to burnout in emergency medicine and medical toxicology, a lack of perceived control over one’s career trajectory may affect women more substantially and stem from gender-based factors hovering beneath the surface of burnout, such as workplace discrimination, pay differentials, role strain, lack of mentorship, and barriers to promotion and other academic opportunities [ 5 , 6 , 10 – 13 ]. Furthermore, women physicians may be more likely to have a partner with a robust enough income to allow for earlier reduction in work hours or retirement [ 14 – 17 ]. While both women and men are subject to at-risk career longevity, women may be more likely to either choose to leave their career and be able to afford to do so [ 15 – 18 ]. Taken together, this body of research suggests that Love’s would-be women senior authors may be canaries in the coal mine: as the modern work environment becomes increasingly challenging, stagnation in female senior authorship may reflect the fact that escalating burnout is universally reducing physician retention and career longevity, and especially that of women. Gender equality and inclusion in authorship may thus be best supported not only by improving gender diversity in medical toxicology at the outset, but by improving career durability in the fields of emergency medicine and medical toxicology writ large. Resolving the problem of field-wide burnout is beyond the scope of this editorial, but we want to note that there have been actions taken within the medical toxicology community to initiate movement towards addressing these issues. These initiatives may in fact have facilitated the positive findings of Love et al. As alumni of the Journal of Medical Toxicology (JMT) Leslie Dye Fellow-in-Training (FIT) program implemented by a female editorial board member in 2010, we commend the efforts of the JMT editorial team to promote early career opportunities and longitudinal career trajectories for women in medical toxicology. Indeed, half of the JMT FIT editors have been women, and those alumni frequently join the editorial board as full members. Today, women constitute over 30% of the editorial board; in fact, most newly recruited board members over the last decade have been women. Engagement of early career physicians via opportunities such as the JMT fellow-in-training program is important to promote gender diversity and inclusion in publishing. If this engagement is both sustained and widespread across academic emergency medicine and medical toxicology, such that individuals are longitudinally mentored and supported, we may be more likely to promote careers that are long enough for mid- and late-career physicians to serve as positive role models for up-and-coming physician scholars. Female authorship is an important marker of the advancement of women in medicine, and Love et al. have found positive signs of growth. Still, much more must be done by health systems and membership organizations to support and develop physicians: more opportunities without support, development, or appreciation will not achieve equality. Acknowledgements The authors would like to thank Dr. Mark Mycyk for his ongoing mentorship. Declarations Conflict of Interest The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Filardo G, Da Graca B, Sass DM, Pollock BD, et al. Trends and comparison of female first authorship in high impact medical journals: observational study (1994–2014). BMJ. 2016;352:i847. 10.1136/bmj.i847. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 2. Abdalla S, Abdalla M, Saad 