INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5019,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5514613','The 10-D assessment and evidence-based medicine tool for authors and peer reviewers in clinical pharmacology .','Woodcock BG, Harder S.','2017','gpp esign iagnoses rug osages iscussion eductions ocumentation eclarations gpp gpp ominous gpp esign iagnoses rug osages iscussion eductions ocumentation eclarations gpp begs enantiomers stereoisomers disprove deductions antipyretics penicillins sulfonamides acetylator gpp gpp esign iagnoses rug osages iscussion eductions ocumentation eclarations gpp bgw inventor constructor woodcock dustri feistle deisenhofen dustri rockledge bgw ijcpt nonregistered ijcpt woodcock luger woodcock pattanaik bies gilchrist isbister rhbnp wmc pirmohamed thong thong vervloet wao worldallergy allergic_diseases_center drugallergy loder groves macauley brit c950','Abstract Background: Peer reviewers and authors of clinical pharmacology manuscripts need to meet the standards for Evidence-Based Medicine (EBM) and Good Publication Practices (GPP), and editors of clinical pharmacology journals have to maintain an overview of the peer review process. Methods and results: The peer review process can be monitored and facilitated using the 10-D assessment, which comprises peer review criteria to determine if: 1. d esign of the study, 2. d iagnoses employed, 3. d rug molecules involved, 4. d osages applied, 5. d ata collected, 6. d iscussion of the findings, 7. d eductions made, 8. d ocumentation, 9. d eclarations, and 10. d HS (drug hypersensitivity syndrome) risk assessment is in accord with the objectives of the study and meet the requirements of EBM and GPP. Conclusions: The 10-D assessment tool, although easy to apply, requires a high level of clinical pharmacology expertise, especially in the fields of drug disposition, pharmacokinetics, and drug action. Its application will facilitate the peer review of clinical research and clinical trial reports and thus promote safety in drug development and pharmacotherapy and meet the needs of Good Publication Practices. Keywords: peer review, clinical pharmacology, 10-D assessment, evidence-based medicine, Good Publication Practices (GPP), registration of observational studies The herein described 10-D assessment tool for peer reviewers, authors, and publishers of clinical pharmacology manuscripts is a further development of the 7-D assessment [ 1 ] and 8-D assessment reported previously [ 2 ]. The need for such a tool was highlighted recently in an article with the rather ominous title “Organized crime against the academic peer review system” [ 3 ]. The problem was that some editors had lost their overview in managing manuscripts submitted to their journals to the extent that some authors had been able to “peer review” their own manuscripts [ 4 ]. A retraction was subsequently published after the article had appeared in print. In the case of clinical pharmacology, peer review criteria should be suitable for manuscripts covering a wide variety of topics, research protocols, and manuscript formats. As well as being clearly defined, comprehensive, and dealing with the aspects important in clinical pharmacology, these criteria must be simple to apply. The publication of clinical findings is a driving force in pharmacotherapy, and therefore the peer review process is a determinant for safety in drug development and pharmacotherapy. The peer review process must therefore meet the requirements of Evidence-Based Medicine (EBM) [ 5 ] and Good Clinical Practices (GPP) [ 1 ]. The 10-D assessment evaluates whether the following criteria: d esign of the study, d iagnoses employed, d rug molecules involved, d osages applied, d ata collected, d iscussion, d eductions made d ocumentation support d eclarations d HS risk assessment are in accord with the objectives of the study, meet the requirements of EBM and Good Publication Practices (GPP) [ 1 ] as well as the criteria for registration and publication of observational studies in humans (see Appendix ) where: Right design means that the study design and protocol are appropriate for answering the question(s) being asked. Right diagnosis is relevant for investigations both in patients and healthy subjects where subject and patient description and patient selection need to be detailed, accurate, and appropriate for the aims of the study. Right drug molecule begs the questions, “Is the active agent a known molecular species?” and “Can the drug entity have a mode of action compatible with the observed pharmacological effects? Does a pharmacological effect observed in vitro have a counterpart in vivo? Do confounding factors, such as the presence of drug enantiomers, stereoisomers, or drug combinations, exist?” Herbal drugs and extracts do not generally fit in with the concepts of EBM. High first-pass effects make it likely that more than one active species is present in the tissues. Right dosage concerns not only the size of the dose (i.e., is the dose or concentration clinically relevant?), but also the method of administration, bioavailability, and duration of treatment. These questions also apply to in vitro studies with tissues and cells. Right data are those data required to meet the objectives of the study, which can establish or disprove efficacy, which have been obtained using state-of-the-art methods, and which have been evaluated using recognized data-analysis procedures. In the case of reviews of the literature, the retrieval methods used and quality of the studies reviewed need to be scrutinized. Right discussion means that all limitations of the study are stated, new findings are highlighted, differences compared to other investigations are discussed satisfactorily, and due recognition is given to the work of earlier investigators in the field. Right deductions means that conclusions are based on a correct and objective interpretation of the research findings and recommendations are made with due caution regarding patient safety and efficacy requirements in clinical pharmacotherapy. Right documentation addresses primarily the quality of the evidence in the supportive literature and asks the questions: “Is the documentation up-to-date? Is it obtained from peer-reviewed sources, and is it comprehensive?” The citation of websites is very useful for providing information but must be viewed with caution when used to provide evidence. Information on websites is not peer reviewed and can be subject to change. Right declarations means that, where appropriate, the research project or clinical trial has been conducted in accord with the Declaration of Helsinki, has been registered by the responsible authority, and registration details are stated in the abstract (see Appendix ), an ethics commission (for human or animal studies), internal review board or external review board has been consulted, patient consent has been obtained correctly, conflicts of interest have been declared, and transparency exists regarding the contribution of individual authors. Right DHS risk assessment addresses the question: “Has the possibility of a DHS (drug hypersensitivity syndrome, either IgE–mediated (immediate) or non-IgE-mediated (delayed), i.e., ADR Type B) that could occur during the study been addressed appropriately?” [ 6 , 7 , 8 ]. The risk can be graded 1 – 3 using: 1. No risk: established drug in a host from a non-risk ethnic group. 2. Moderate risk: a) innovative drug, or b) established drug from a risk category, or c) established drug used in a host from a risk ethnic group. 3. High risk: a) innovative drug or b) drug from a risk category used in a host from a risk ethnic group and taking into account: Drug Factors: nature of the drug, degree of exposure (dose, duration, frequency), route of administration, cross-sensitization. Drugs frequently associated with DHS are aspirin (other analgesics-antipyretics), penicillins and cephalosporins, sulfonamides, and anticonvulsants. and Host factors: age and sex, genetic factors (HLA type, acetylator status), concurrent medical illness (e.g., Epstein-Barr virus (EBV), human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), asthma), previous drug reaction, multiple allergy syndrome. If the findings regarding any one of these assessment criteria are questionable, the compliance of the research with EBM and GPP principles is weakened, and the reviewers and editors will make recommendations accordingly. Conclusion The 10-D assessment, a tool to assist authors and peer reviewers of clinical pharmacology manuscripts to meet the requirements for GPP and EBM and to help editors of clinical pharmacology journals maintain an overview of the peer review process has been described. It comprises peer review criteria to determine if the: 1. d esign of the study, 2. d iagnoses employed, 3. d rug molecules involved, 4. d osages applied, 5. d ata collected, 6. d iscussion of the findings, 7. d eductions made, 8. d ocumentation supporting the work, 9. d eclarations concerning ethical and transparency questions, and 10. d HS risk assessment is in accord with the objectives of the study and meet the requirements of EBM and GPP. This tool, although easy to apply, requires a high level of clinical pharmacology expertise, especially in the fields of drug disposition, pharmacokinetics, and drug action. Its implementation will improve standards in publishing clinical pharmacological research, and patient safety will be impacted by this process. Role of authors BGW is the inventor and constructor of the 10-D assessment concept and is responsible for the composition of this report. SB has given input to the report concerning specific clinical and scientific aspects relevant to the implementation of these peer review guidelines by peer reviewers, review boards, and ethics commissions and regarding the accuracy of the content in the report in general. Conflict of interest Barry Woodcock is Editor-in-Chief of the International Journal of Clinical Pharmacology and Therapeutics published by Dustri-Verlag Dr. Karl Feistle GmbH & Co. KG, Munich-Deisenhofen, Germany, and Dustri-Verlag Inc., Rockledge, FL. USA. Appendix Registration and publication of observational studies in the International Journal of Clinical Pharmacology and Therapeutics Policy Statement: 2015/JF/BGW based on [ 9 ]. a) Current policy of the International Journal of Clinical Pharmacology and Therapeutics (IJCPT) permits the publication of registered and nonregistered observational studies.* b) Authors submitting observational studies for publication in IJCPT are asked to observe the recommendations made in the STROBE statement [ 10 ]. The STROBE recommendations are aimed at improving the clarity of study reporting. c) Authors are therefore required to describe in their papers exactly what they did during their studies and to explain the scientific background and rationale for the investigation being reported. Information should be provided on: i) Origins, motivations, and data interrogation methods used in the work (where applicable). ii) The study hypothesis: If the study hypotheses were developed after carrying out the investigation, authors will need to a) explain the steps taken to minimize bias, b) provide study protocols if they exist. *Under review (at June 2017) References 1. Woodcock BG Luger V Good publication practices in clinical pharmacology: transparency, evidence-based medicine and the 7-D assessment. Int J Clin Pharmacol Ther. 2015; 53: 799–802. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Woodcock BG Peer review in Clinical Pharmacology using the 8-D Assessment. Int J Clin Pharmacol Ther. 2017; 55: 201–200. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Cohen A Pattanaik S Kumar P Bies RR de Boer A Ferro A Gilchrist A Isbister GK Ross S Webb AJ Organised crime against the academic peer review system. Br J Clin Pharmacol. 2016 2016; 81: 1012–1017. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Lv MY Deng SL Long XF Retraction. rhBNP therapy can improve clinical outcomes and reduce in-hospital mortality compared with dobutamine in heart failure patients: a meta-analysis. Br J Clin Pharmacol. 2016; 81: 174–185. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [ Retracted ] 5. Sackett DL Rosenberg WMC Gray JA Haynes RB Richardson WS Evidence based medicine: what it is and what it isn’t. BMJ. 1996; 312: 71–72. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Pirmohamed M Park BK Adverse drug reactions: back to the future. Br J Clin Pharmacol. 2003; 55: 486–492. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Thong BY Tan TC Epidemiology and risk factors for drug allergy. Br J Clin Pharmacol. 2011; 71: 684–700. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Thong BY Vervloet D Drug Allergies: WAO Review (Posted 2007; Updated 2014; Accessed 09.05.2017). http://www.worldallergy.org/professional/allergic_diseases_center/drugallergy/ 2017. 9. Loder E Groves T Macauley D Registration of observational studies. Brit Med J. 2010; 340: c950. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. von Elm E Altman DG Egger M Pocock SJ Gøtzsche PC Vandenbroucke JP Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology (STROBE) statement: guidelines for reporting observational studies. BMJ. 2007; 335: 806–808. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',4,'2025-10-15 10:26:32','2025-10-15 10:26:32');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5020,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2749666','Quality assurance for kilo- and megavoltage in-room imaging and localization for off- and online setup error correction.','Balter JM, Antonuk LE.','2008','igrt imager megavoltage megavoltage predates imagers amfpis imager imager imagers amfpi photodiode transistor tft amfpi linac preamplifiers linac synchronization imager imager amfpi megavoltage radiopaque megavoltage amfpi amfpi imager fiducial amfpi detectability amfpi equalization blur aesthetically pleasing imager imager imager imager isocenter insure isocenter subsystems isocenter positionings isocenter isocenter isocenter isocenter isocenter tighter imager megavoltage equalization equalization isocenter isocenter isocenter offsets isocenter megavoltage rectangular isocenteric debugging anthropomorphic delves adoptions tradeoffs drrs negate anthropomorphic igrt accelerators nal outlying imager interlocks isocenter fueled igrt outpaced epids srao00500115 antonuk r31 antonuk megavoltage hydrogenated seppi rajapakshe luchka shalev imagers doorn bezak epid sonke herk accelerators leszczynski shalev cosby mcshan balter jacmp v3i3 gilhuijs gilhuijs gilhuijs herk gilhuijs herk s0360 remeijer s0360 herk heijmen s0360','Abstract In-room radiography is not a new concept for image guided radiation therapy (IGRT). The rapid advances of technology, however, have made this positioning method convenient, and thus radiograph-based positioning has propagated widely. The paradigms for quality assurance of radiograph-based positioning include imager performance, systems integration, infrastructure, procedure documentation and testing, and support for positioning strategy implementation. Introduction While a large variety of volumetric imaging systems have been introduced recently, the vast majority of current image guidance involves the use of radiographic imaging information to drive verification of treatment. While imaging technology is evolving, the general problems of radiographic use for localization have been extensively studied, and thus quite mature guidance is available for proper quality assurance of technology and procedure for in-room radiography. Many of the issues associated with megavoltage imaging were addressed by the American Association of Physicists in Medicine. In the report of task group 58[ 1 ], the general problems of gantry-mounted megavoltage radiographic systems for setup verification were elucidated. The report predates the advent of active matrix flat panel imagers (AMFPIs), by far the dominant current acquisition system for kV and MV radiographs in treatment rooms. Furthermore, most of the concepts of data integration needed to smoothly support in-room localization did not exist at the time of its writing. Nonetheless, this report serves as a valuable reference, which details many of the quality assurance considerations that are still relevant and which will be expanded on here. The general quality assurance paradigm for radiograph-based setup error correction can be broken down into the following components: equipment performance (image quality, dose), systems integration (accuracy, calibration), and process implementation (infrastructure and protocol development and implementation). Equipment Performance The components of a radiographic localization system include: an imager, imager mount, image processing and display software, image alignment tools, and connections with an image and patient record database. This paper will focus on flat panel imaging systems since other systems such as camera-mirror and liquid ion chamber imagers have previously been reported in considerable detail[ 2 – 4 ], and their prevalence is expected to diminish steadily over time. The unique and essential component of an AMFPI consists of a two-dimensional grid of imaging pixels, each of which comprises an amorphous silicon photodiode coupled to a thin-film transistor (TFT). The pixels are addressed and read out by means of two orthogonal sets of control lines that extend to contacts on the periphery of the glass substrate on which the array is fabricated[ 5 ]. There are two possible read out modes for these imaging systems: radiographic and fluoroscopic. Early MV AMFPI systems tended to produce images with groups of consecutive rows of pixels at slightly higher signal levels than nearby rows, an artifact commonly referred to as banding. Such banding can occur due to variation in linac output (during fluoroscopic operation) or variable gain in the preamplifiers that receive the pixel signals from the data line. Modern, more sophisticated systems should reduce or eliminate such effects through methods such as frame averaging and application of image processing corrections based on previously acquired dark and flood frames. The presence of banding in images could be indicative of unusual variations in linac output (for fluoroscopic acquisition), lack of synchronization between imager operation and beam delivery, or problems associated with image processing (for either mode). Image Quality While phantoms have been designed for portal imaging, the most common form, a contrast-detail phantom (e.g., the “las vegas” phantom)[ 1 , 6 , 7 ] was developed in an era in which imager sensitivity and artifacts limited usefulness of images in resolving anatomical features. The dramatic improvement in image quality provided by AMFPI systems has limited the usefulness of these early phantoms, and currently source size and contrast limits imposed by Compton scattering heavily influence the degree to which anatomy can be resolved for purposes of patient positioning. Other phantoms were developed around the concept of contrast-to-noise ratio (CNR), as well as high-contrast spatial resolution[ 8 ]. These phantoms may be more applicable to modern megavoltage imaging. A major factor in image quality related to detection of implanted radiopaque markers with megavoltage AMFPI systems involves the frequency of pixel defects. Typically, AMFPI systems have a number of isolated pixels, pixel clusters, and even one or more lines of pixels that are either completely non-responsive, or do not otherwise respond in a normal manner to incident radiation. Some of these defects may be transient, while others are permanent. Generally, these pixel and line defects are identified through acquisition of flood and dark fields during routine quality assurance. Once identified (e.g. in a bad pixel map), the imager software will typically fill in information for bad pixels using either a neighboring pixel/line value, or an average of surrounding pixels. In either case, the major impact of large pixel cluster defects, or consecutive line defects will be increased difficulty in localizing small implanted fiducial markers. Knowing the fraction of bad pixels and their relative locations in the imaging field can help determine the impact on marker localization. Diagnostic AMFPI imaging involves much higher subject contrast information, and thus more common radiographic image quality phantoms may be appropriate. At present, the demands for low contrast detectability in patient positioning probably do not match those of diagnostic radiology, although more advanced image analysis tools may increase demands on image quality. It is likely that the most demanding component of in-room radiographic use, in terms of image quality, is cone beam CT imaging, discussed elsewhere in this issue. A recent, multiple institution review of early users of one of the commercial diagnostic systems documents early experience, with summaries of some of the key components of the systems for radiographic as well as tomographic image guidance[ 9 ]. Dose In general, a modern AMFPI should permit MV radiography with 3 monitor units, or less. Averaging of multiple frames may be employed, with potential advantages of reduced noise and (in fluoroscopic mode) equalization of exposure, and thus background contrast, across an array. The cost of averaging is increased dose, as well as the potential for movement-related blur. It is important to keep the purpose and value of the image in context, so that excess dose is not given to the patient in search of images that may be more aesthetically pleasing, and yet may not provide any added useful information for setup verification . The doses for radiographic localization using diagnostic x-rays are far lower than for MV imaging, and thus dose may be considered negligible by some users. However, it is important to not simply accept this fact and ignore imaging doses, since increased frequency of imaging, as well as potential developments in treatment monitoring through fluoroscopy, may result in very high (skin) doses over time. This point was recently addressed by AAPM task group 75 (report pending)[ 10 ]. It is recommended that techniques be established and related exposures measured at the time of commissioning a diagnostic radiographic localization or monitoring procedure. Systems Integration The usefulness of radiographic imaging in the setup verification process is highly dependent on the systems developed around the imager. The components that contribute to a typical system consist of an imager, control software, imager positioning system, possible source positioning system, image display and analysis software, and connections to patient information and treatment control systems. The proper design and interaction of these systems need to be understood to develop a functional, and useful system for treatment verification. One example is related to the imager (and source) positioning systems. Room-mounted x-ray sources and detectors have the advantage of a fixed geometric relationship to isocenter. With these systems, relatively simple quality assurance checks can be performed to insure that this relationship is maintained. Gantry-mounted imaging (and X-ray) systems need arms to position the system in a state that projects through isocenter. Due to the weight of the gantry and subsystems, the position of the detector (and source) relative to isocenter may change as a function of gantry angle, and may or may not be reproducible on multiple positionings[ 11 ]. This variation needs to be explored upon acceptance and commissioning of a system. The next best thing to a system that maintains a perfect relation to isocenter is one in which the varying relationship is reproducible, characterized, and integrated into software so that it does not negatively impact the relative measurement of position in the treatment room. Geometric phantoms are typically used to establish the relationship of isocenter to its projection. In addition to absolute checks, relative checks (e.g. kV vs MV, anterior versus lateral projections) should be evaluated. In fact, an absolute check may be difficult, as standard quality assurance typically ensures that the lasers indicate isocenter to within 2 mm. A room with isocenter maintained to stereotactic specifications may have a mechanical “standard” for isocenter that has tighter specifications, and which may aid in imager calibration. Of note, the focal spot of a linear accelerator has also been shown to potentially move, thus further complicating positioning accuracy[ 12 ]. Software tools should include an infrastructure for proper image display and analysis. Image display tools may include enhancement. For megavoltage imaging, there is limited subject contrast between bone and soft tissue. Typically applied enhancement algorithms include edge enhancement and histogram equalization. Excessive use of these methods may in fact obscure anatomy, due to the high noise content, structured noise/artifacts (e.g. from the treatment table, masks, etc.), and potential for excessive contrast enhancement (e.g. from adaptive histogram equalization) to confound the accuracy of subsequent position measurement[ 13 ]. Image analysis may include a series of measurement tools for estimating the distance of anatomic landmarks from the field edge or isocenter. The most critical infrastructure in this area is the availability of image alignment tools. The ability to align the reference model of the patient to the sampled anatomy from a portal or other localization radiograph is critical for efficient on-line or off-line setup verification. While numerous automated alignment tools have been investigated, such methods have generally only worked robustly for fixed anatomy or simplified surrogate anatomic features such as implanted markers. Overall, manual alignment tools such as template or image overlay matching, are very valuable to the infrastructure for position verification[ 3 , 14 – 22 ]. The process of verification involves two steps: establishment of the room coordinate system relative to the image(s), and establishment of the patient position relative to the same image(s), and thus, to isocenter. Routine quality assurance should maintain the known isocenter position, as well as ability to measure known offsets, in a simplified phantom. It is advisable to test this relationship at several gantry angles, especially those at which image alignment is routinely performed. The advance of complex treatments using multiple beam angles has made portal-based positioning very complex, and thus a very popular paradigm involves localization through a pair of orthogonal radiographs, (e.g. an anterior and lateral projection). For such localization, a treatment portal may not be available, and thus the relationship to isocenter may not have a ready secondary check provided by the shaped field border. One option is to expose the (megavoltage) images with a square or rectangular field of a known size. In this manner, the projection of the field borders may serve as a secondary check of the isocenteric coordinate system. Database integration is quite common with modern in-room imaging systems. The major issues are storage and labeling of images, and ability to archive information. The chain of treatment verification Outside of the equipment and tools available at the treatment unit, the overall process of patient positioning from simulation forward through treatment needs to be evaluated and optimized to permit efficient and accurate use of radiographic images for positioning and verification. A very important step in the implementation of radiograph-based (or any other) image guidance is an end-to-end test. This process will permit debugging and documenting all steps from simulation through planning and treatment-based positioning. While mechanically engineered phantoms are useful for these tests, an anthropomorphic phantom may further elucidate factors such as patient size and image quality and their impact on protocols for positioning. Positioning strategies The paper by Yan et al in this issue delves deeply into the concept of adaptive therapy. This is a very powerful philosophy for managing the impact of variations on treatment. Prior to wide-scale adoptions of the concepts presented therein, simpler paradigms, such as on-line and off-line adjustment, can still lead to significant improvements in position. These paradigms require process implementation (and thus process QA), may not be completely supported by various software platforms, and have tradeoffs in accuracy versus effort that need to be explored. On-line adjustment The prevalent use of radiograph-based guidance is for on-line adjustment. Simply put, this involves acquiring radiographs, comparing them to reference information (radiographs, DRRs, reference anatomic templates, reference CT volumes for 2D–3D matching), and using the measurements from these comparisons to adjust the position of the patient immediately. On-line adjustment can be applied to all measurements, or with a threshold (i.e. adjustments will not be made for transformations below threshold values). The reasons for applying a threshold are twofold: 1) adjustments and potential subsequent verification may take time/dose, and 2) error in measurement and correction may, at some level, negate the benefit of adjustment in the first place. While theoretical studies have begun to elucidate this cost-benefit relationship, a practical threshold for current radiograph-based positioning is likely in the 2 to 3 mm range. This can be demonstrated by repeating the end-to-end test with an anthropomorphic phantom, and plotting the range of resulting position variations (image-based positioning versus an absolute reference marked on the phantom). On-line adjustment reduces systematic as well as random error. If no threshold is applied, it is expected that the final error is a combination of errors in measurement and correction, as well as subsequent patient movement. While outside the scope of this paper, immobilization becomes a key concept in IGRT, and one that needs further exploration and optimization. Off-line adjustment Off-line adjustment is primarily aimed at reducing systematic errors. It is important to note that these errors arise not only from mechanical and calibration differences between CT scanners and linear accelerators, but from the random variation of patient position as well. This is easily seen by noting that the patient is typically not at his/her “average” position at simulation, as the random component of their position is sampled each time they are immobilized. There are a number of off-line strategies. A common strategy is the no-action-level (NAL) strategy[ 23 ]. In this strategy, the average position after a small number (3–5) of fractions is corrected. It has been shown that the mean position of a patient can be estimated with reasonable accuracy after a small number of measurements, for a majority of patients. It is important, however, to have some check, even with reduced frequency, in place to catch outlying patients. In addition, a less accurate estimation of random error can be made in the first 3 to 5 measurements. If random error is unexpectedly large, then a decision can be made to switch to on-line localization on a case-by-case basis. It is especially in these instances that a more global philosophy guiding imaging and adjustment decisions is needed, and where adaptive strategies have significant advantages. Implementation of off-line strategies is very cumbersome with existing software platforms. Very few information systems permit tracking of position over multiple fractions and combining this information, and thus outside tools such as custom software, spreadsheets, and hand-entered forms are used. This challenge requires significant attention be paid to process quality assurance, and represents an area in which significant infrastructure changes should occur in the future. QA guidelines While specific guidelines are beyond the scope of this paper, a few brief summary points can be made. The QA program for radiograph-based positioning (kV and MV) should include the following: Commissioning/acceptance tests: Image quality and dose Imager positioning accuracy and reproducibility Safety systems (e.g. collision interlocks) Software tools for image display, analysis, archival, and review End-to-end test of positioning Documentation of processes for use (SOPs) Routine quality assurance Image quality, especially extent of masked bad pixels/lines Accuracy (distance to agreement) of isocenter Positioning strategies Departmental review of protocols and training Development of ancillary tools for documenting protocol-based positioning (e.g., forms, spreadsheets) Periodic review of frequencies of adjustments, accuracies of resulting positions, and guidance for future patient/protocol selection Summary Radiograph-based image guidance has gone through a rapid period of expansion after nearly 2 decades of slow development. This has been fueled by dramatic improvements in image quality and software infrastructure. The quality assurance paradigms for this IGRT implementation, while reasonable in some areas, have otherwise been ill-defined (e.g. process QA for positioning strategies) or outpaced by technology (e.g. image quality for MV radiography). The development of end-to-end tests, as well as the involvement of a broad cross section of staff involved in these procedures, especially for initial process development, documentation, and feedback, is crucial to safe and effective implementation of radiograph-based positioning. While small, dosimetric issues from using these systems cannot be ignored. Footnotes Publisher''s Disclaimer: This is a PDF file of an unedited manuscript that has been accepted for publication. As a service to our customers we are providing this early version of the manuscript. The manuscript will undergo copyediting, typesetting, and review of the resulting proof before it is published in its final citable form. Please note that during the production process errors may be discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the journal pertain. Bibliography 1. Herman MG, et al. Clinical use of electronic portal imaging: report of AAPM Radiation Therapy Committee Task Group 58. Med Phys. 2001;28(5):712–37. doi: 10.1118/1.1368128. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Boyer AL, et al. A review of electronic portal imaging devices (EPIDs) Med Phys. 1992;19(1):1–16. doi: 10.1118/1.596878. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Munro P. Portal Imaging Technology: Past, Present, and Future. Semin Radiat Oncol. 1995;5(2):115–133. doi: 10.1054/SRAO00500115. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Antonuk LE. Electronic portal imaging devices: a review and historical perspective of contemporary technologies and research. Phys Med Biol. 2002;47(6):R31–65. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Antonuk LE, et al. Demonstration of megavoltage and diagnostic x-ray imaging with hydrogenated amorphous silicon arrays. Med Phys. 1992;19(6):1455–66. doi: 10.1118/1.596802. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Kubo HD, Shapi