n disproportionally fracturedetect gleamer boneview osteodetect boneview guermazi fracturedetect osteodetect osteodetect cropped rescaled rayvolve koala imagebiopsy kellgren osteophyte koala populates verbiage kellgren dictation lucencies nehrer koala koala osteophyte oarsi koala radiobotics rbknee kellgren rbknee kellgren rbknee tonnis radiobotics rbhip columbo headquartered reformatting conformite kellgren k23ns118056 doris yaeger yaniv oermann hooghe gielen remoortel beadnall hecke ribbens billiet fragoso wille nielson durgerian loewenstein e101 decarie nadeau finana denby wieshmann byun neuropsychiatr thoprakarn gourieux rozema kruitbosch minnen dorgelo kraeima ooijen pma midfacial yanagawa denoising rozema kruitbosch minnen dorgelo kraeima ooijen pma midfacial brendlin plajer chaika denoising brendlin plajer denoising pixelshine kolk bym slotman nijholt pexman heit demchuk amukotuwa straka shalitin sudry mates golan jestm ringheanu tekle e000224 fernholm pukk harenstam wachtler holzmann hotchkiss lindsey daluiski sassoon kellgren varacallo segond nehrer ljuhar steindl 957s 65s brejnebøl nybing kovalenko bremjit tonnis juang','Abstract There is a rapidly increasing number of artificial intelligence (AI) products cleared by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for quantification, identification, and even diagnosis in clinical radiology. This review article aims to summarize the landscape of current commercial software products in neuroimaging and musculoskeletal radiology. We will discuss key applications, provide an overview of current FDA cleared products, and summarize relevant peer reviewed publications of these products when available. Introduction Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly used to enhance the capability, efficiency, and accuracy of radiologists. With the recognition of software as a medical device, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has approved almost 200 algorithms through the 501(k) Class II premarket approval pathway, and many of these approvals pertain to neuroimaging and musculoskeletal radiology. 1 Many AI technologies are gradually integrated into daily clinical practice. Therefore, it is important to not only be aware of the variety of tools available but also to evaluate the original validation data and compare their results with studies reporting real-time use of AI in populations and care settings, which may differ from the development data. While the first AI-based product was approved in 2006 ( Figure 1 ), there has been a recent surge in approval of clinical tools to accelerate and enhance diagnosis and treatment since 2018. This review aims to summarize existing FDA-approved AI products in neuroimaging and musculoskeletal radiology and some of the peer-reviewed studies evaluating these technologies. The focus of this review will pertain to two areas, the first is applications of AI in neuroradiology, with examples of quantification, image optimization, improved visualization, diagnosis, decision assistance, and triage ( Table 1 ). The second area focuses on musculoskeletal applications of AI software, including fractures detection, diagnosis and quantification of knee arthritis, and quantification of spinal degenerative disease ( Table 1 ). When available, the peer reviewed literature providing the evaluation of the AI products are described and cited. Figure 1. Open in a new tab FDA approvals by year over time as of August 2022. Table 1. Lists all FDA-approved imaging products pertaining to Neuroradiology and Musculoskeletal radiology as of August 2022 and their respective approval dates, country, state, and city where they are headquartered. Company Product Function Modality Headquarters Month, Year of Approval Website Viz.AI LVO Computer Assisted Detection CTA San Francisco, California, USA February, 2018 https://www.viz.ai/lvo-ctp Viz.AI CT Perfusion Computer Assisted Detection CTP San Francisco, California, USA February, 2018 https://www.viz.ai/lvo-ctp Viz.AI Aneurysm Computer Assisted Detection CTA San Francisco, California, USA February, 2022 https://www.viz.ai/aneurysm Viz.AI ICH Computer Assisted Detection CT San Francisco, California, USA February, 2022 https://www.viz.ai/viz-ich MaxQ AI Accipio Ix Computer Assisted Detection CT Tel Aviv, Israel November, 2018 https://www.maxq.ai/products icometrix NV Icobrain MS Quantification MR Leuven, Belguim October, 2018 https://icometrix.com/services/icobrain-ms icometrix NV Icobrain DM Quantification CT, MR Leuven, Belguim November, 2018 https://icometrix.com/services/icobrain-dm icometrix NV Icobrain TBI Quantification CT, MR Leuven, Belguim November, 2018 https://icometrix.com/services/icobrain-tbi icometrix NV Icobrain CVA Quantification CT Leuven, Belguim August, 2016 https://icometrix.com/services/icobrain-cva icometrix NV Icobrain EP Quantification MR Leuven, Belguim August, 2016 https://icometrix.com/services/icobrain-ep Subtle Medical, Inc SubtleMR Quantification MR Menlo Park, California, USA July, 2019 https://subtlemedical.com/subtlemr/ iSchemaView, Inc. Rapid CTP Quantification CTP Menlo Park, California, USA December, 2018 https://www.rapidai.com/ iSchemaView,Inc. Rapid MRI Quantification MRI Menlo Park, California, USA December, 2018 https://www.rapidai.com/ iSchemaView, Inc. Rapid ASPECTS Computer Assisted Detection CT Menlo Park, California, USA June, 2020 https://www.rapidai.com/ iSchemaView, Inc. Rapid ICH Computer Assisted Diagnosis CT Menlo Park, California, USA March, 2020 https://www.rapidai.com/ iSchemaView, Inc. Rapid CTA Quantification CTA Menlo Park, California, USA May, 2018 https://www.rapidai.com/ iSchemaView, Inc. Rapid Aneurysm Quantification CTA Menlo Park, California, USA May, 2018 https://www.rapidai.com/ Quantib Quantib ND Quantification MRI Rotterdam, Netherlands December, 2018 https://www.quantib.com/solutions/quantib-nd Quantib Quantib Brain Quantification MRI Rotterdam, Netherlands September, 2017 https://www.quantib.com/solutions/partnerships/quantib-brain AlgoMedica PixelShine Quantification CT Sunnyvale, California, USA June, 2016 https://www.algomedica.com/low-radation-ct-scans-algomedica Omniscient Neurotech Quicktome Quantification MR Sydney, Australia February, 2021 https://quicktome.o8t.com/ Aidoc Medical LVO Computer Assisted Diagnosis CTA Tel Aviv, Israel January, 2020 https://www.aidoc.com/radiology-ai/ Aidoc Medical Cervical Spine Computer Assisted Diagnosis CT Tel Aviv, Israel June, 2019 https://www.aidoc.com/radiology-ai/ Aidoc Medical RibFx Computer Assisted Diagnosis CT Tel Aviv, Israel April, 2021 https://www.aidoc.com/radiology-ai/ ImageBiopsy Lab Koala Computer Assisted Detection XR Vienna, Austria November, 2019 https://www.imagebiopsy.com/ Imagen Osteodetect Computer Assisted Diagnosis XR New York, NY, USA May, 2018 https://imagen.ai/ai-software/ Imagen FractureDetect Computer Assisted Detection XR New York, NY, USA July, 2020 https://imagen.ai/ai-software/ Gleamer BoneView Computer Assisted Detection XR Paris, France March, 2020 https://www.gleamer.ai/solutions/boneview/ SmartSoft Healthcare CoLumbo Computer Assisted Diagnosis MR Varna, Bulgaria June, 2022 https://columbo.me/ GrayLight Sens.ai Computer Assisted Detection MR Gliwice, Poland March, 2020 https://graylight-imaging.com/brain-tumor-segmentation-challenges-graylight-imagings-success/ Cercare Medical Cercare Perfusion Quantification MR Aarhus, Denmark November, 2019 https://cercare-medical.com/ Nanox.AI Neuro Solution Computer Assisted Detection CT Kibbutz Shefayim, Israel May, 2020 https://www.nanox.vision/ai Advantis Brainance MD Quantification MR Eindhoven, Netherlands March, 2018 https://advantis.io/ Cortechs.ai NeuroQuant Quantification MR San Diego, California, United States August, 2006 https://cortechs.ai/ Qure.ai qER Quantification CT Mumba, India May, 2018 https://qure.ai/ Medimaps TBS iNsight (Osteo) Quantification XR (DXA) Geneva, Switzerland April, 2019 https://www.medimapsgroup.com/tbs-osteo/ Brainreader Neuroreader Quantification MR Aalborg, Denmark August, 2015 https://brainreader.net/the-neuroreader-report/ Imaging Biometrics IB Delta Suite Quantification MR Elm Grove, Wisconsin, United States January, 2013 https://www.imagingbiometrics.com/what-we-offer/product-services/ib-neuro/ SyntheticMR SyMRI NEURO Quantification MR Linkoping, Sweden June, 2019 https://syntheticmr.com/products/symri-neuro/ AZmed Rayvolve Computer Assisted Diagnosis XR Paris, France June, 2019 https://azmed.co/ Deep01 Limited DeepCT 32 Computer Assisted Detection CT Tapei, Taiwan July, 2019 https://www.deep01.com/ Combinostics cNeuro cMRI Quantification MR Tampere, Finland January, 2017 https://www.combinostics.com/ Arterys Neuro AI Computer Assisted Detection CT, MR San Francisco, California, United States December, 2020 https://www.arterys.com/ Avicenna.AI CINA-ICH Computer Assisted Detection CT La Ciotat, France February, 2020 https://avicenna.ai/ Qynapse QyScore Quantification MR, PET Paris, France December, 2019 https://qynapse.com/ Radiobotics RBknee Computer Assisted Diagnosis XR Copenhagen, Denmark November, 2019 https://radiobotics.com/ Nicolab StrokeViewer Computer Assisted Diagnosis CT Amsterdam, Netherlands December, 2019 https://www.nicolab.com/strokeviewer/ai-portfolio/ MRIguidance BoneMRI Quantification MR Utretch, Netherlands January, 2022 https://mriguidance.com/ Siemens Healthineers AI-Rad Companion Brain MR Quantification MR Erlangen, Germany April, 2022 https://www.siemens-healthineers.com/en-us/digital-health-solutions/digital-solutions-overview/clinical-decision-support/ai-rad-companion Open in a new tab CT: computed tomography, CTA; computed tomography angiography, CTP: computed tomography perfusion, XR: X-ray, MRI: magnetic resonance imaging. Tan highlights indicate musculoskeletal radiology applications while non-highlighted products pertain to neuroradiology Applications of AI in neuroradiology Quantification AI programs have the capability to make automated and reproducible measurements and generate quantitative volumetric data from brain CT and MRI within minutes of image acquisition. The Icometrix’s portfolio of products have different areas of specialization; however, they all share a basis in quantifying the area and/or volume of brain regions to inform clinical care, and track disease processes over time using brain CT or MR. Their volumetric measurements have proven correlations with multiple sclerosis, 2 – 4 dementia, 5 , 6 and epilepsy, 7 , 8 which can guide radiologists, neurologists, and neurosurgeons. Other products that aim to automatically quantify brain regions and pathologic changes (such as white matter hyperintensity) include NeuroQuant, 9 Quantib ND and Brain and Qnapse. 10 The shared value proposition of these types of AI products is in quick quantification and comparison of patient-specific brain region measurements to those of reference populations or prior scans from the same patient, and presenting the information in a visually compelling way which is easily understandable by physicians and patients. While quantification does not aim to detect, diagnose, or prognosticate, the quantitative AI products are easily integrated with clinical workflows and add valuable information through automated segmentation and measurements to inform clinical practice and patient care. Image Optimization An example of image optimization is AlgoMedica’s PixelShine ™ technology which denoises low-dose CT scans. The FDA recommends the use of as low a dose of radiation as reasonably achievable, and AI technologies have the potential to enhance details and clarity of lower resolution and lower radiation scans without losing diagnostic utility. Several recent studies have used cadaveric models, 11 simulated degradation models, and real-time clinical comparisons. In a study by Hata et al that illustrates the benefit of denoising, 41 patients with a total of 252 lung nodules underwent both an ultra-low-dose CT and standard-dose CT scan with the ultra-low-dose CT using 3% of the mSv of radiation compared to standard-dose scan. 12 The images that were reconstructed using deep-learning based denoising and model-based iterative reconstruction had significantly improved subjective scoring on image quality when compared to other low-dose images, and with highest interrater agreement on Lung-RADS evaluation of lung nodules with standard dose images. Although the early study pertains to lungs, others have applied similar AI-based denoising software in non-contrast head CT, 13 as well as whole body CT images, 14 pediatric chest imaging, 15 and pelvic arterial phase CTs. 16 Improved Visualization A series of AI-based software improve visualization of volumetric scans via 3D reconstruction or generation of synthetic CT images from MRI scans. For example, Rapid AI’s Rapid CTA product removes overlying brain parenchyma in CTA scans so that the vessel outlines can be studied and rotated in three-dimensional space to facilitate visualization of cerebral vasculature. MRIguidance BoneMRI ™ uses MRI images to reconstruct bony structures for 3D visualization. Considering that patients may only obtain an MRI before certain procedures such as spinal decompression and/or fusion, a 3D reconstruction of the bony anatomy has the potential to save time and resources as well as spare patients from CT scan radiation. Moreover, certain populations such as pediatric patients and pregnant women may preferentially receive an MRI to avoid exposure to ionizing radiation. A recent study found that the synthetic CT scans generated from MR images was non-inferior to traditional CT in the cervical spine for bone quality, artifact, intervertebral joints, and neuroforamina, and was deemed acceptable in over 90% of cases. 17 Diagnosis, decision assistance, and triage AI programs can be designed to intake information, perform measurements, and segmentation to detect and diagnose areas of concern. While the majority of software applied for detection of intracerebral hemorrhage, large vessel occlusion, and aneurysms are primarily approved for triage purposes by FDA, some AI products are specifically approved for diagnostic applications. For example, Nicolab Nicobrain ™ is the first FDA approved product for the AI diagnosis of acute ischemic infarct. On the other hand, several products automatically quantify the 10-point Alberta Stroke Program Early CT Scores (ASPECTS) to inform eligibility for mechanical thrombectomy intervention depending on areas of grey-white matter differentiation. 18 Triage notification tools aim to pre-screen, triage, and prioritize patients’ scans within the worklist to ensure concerning studies are read first. In neuroradiology, this is commonly applied to acute brain bleeds on non-contrast head CT, and large vessel occlusions identified on CT angiography. Viz AI’s ICH product, iSchemaView’s Rapid ICH ™ , and Avicenna.AI CINA-ICH represent examples of AI application for detection and prioritization of non-contrast brain CT scans to accelerate identification of acute intracerebral hemorrhage. A study comparing RapidAI ICH to a consensus detection of ICH on 308 scans reported sensitivity and specificity >95%. 19 The mainstay of treatment in patients with large vessel occlusion (LVO) stroke is prompt mechanical thrombectomy; however, timely identification is critical in preserving brain function and potential for neurologic recovery. 20 Viz AI’s Large Vessel Occlusion (LVO ™ ) product, iSchemaView’s RAPID AI LVO, 21 MaxQ’s Accipio Ix ™ , and Avicenna.AI CINA-LVO all utilize CTA scans to detect and prioritize scans with evidence of occlusion to expedite care coordination with neuro-interventionalists. Viz.AI demonstrated a sensitivity of 96.3% and specificity of 93.8% 22 in detection of LVO, and 86.7 minutes faster arrival to puncture time after AI implementation compared to before implemenatation. 23 Applications of AI in musculoskeletal radiology Fracture Detection Missed fractures on plain radiographs account for nearly one fourth of all diagnostic errors, with fractures in the hand and wrist being disproportionally represented among missed fracture diagnoses. 24 Imagen FractureDetect ™ and AI Gleamer BoneView ™ and OsteoDetect ™ are examples of products that aim to assist in fracture detection from plain radiographs. For BoneView ™ , a study by Guermazi et al examined 480 patients with total of 350 fractures (30 x-rays with and without fractures for 8 body regions) and reported a 64.8% sensitivity without AI assistance and 75.2% sensitivity with AI assistance and a corresponding specificity of 90.6% without AI and 95.6% with AI, respectively. These accuracy improvements were achieved with a significantly shorter reading time by over six seconds. For FractureDetect, the underlying data for approval was a study of 16,019 radiographs across 15 centers. 25 The OsteoDetect ™ software (from Imagen company) specifically focuses on wrist fractures, and was established following a study examining 31,490 wrist radiographs with a neural network. 26 Of note, OsteoDetect ™ is able to detect the presence or absence of the fracture and overlay a confidence heat-map indicating the location of fracture which can direct the radiologist to fracture location. Moreover, it is important to clarify that for each radiograph, there is a critical pre-processing step whereby radiographs are rotated, cropped, and rescaled to a standard resolution before applying the neural network or AI. These steps are often integrated into the software packages and are essential to a high-fidelity product. Another fracture detection software is Rayvolve ( Table 1 ), however, there is less literature evaluating the product at this time. Diagnosis and quantification of osteoarthritis Knee Osteoarthritis Labeling Assistant (KOALA ™ ) by ImageBiopsy Lab is an example of another AI product that aims to streamline scoring and reporting of common chronic diseases such as osteoarthritis based on standing AP radiographs of the knee. The classical Kellgren-Lawrence criteria of knee osteoarthritis as defined by joint space narrowing, osteophyte formation, and sclerosis, 27 is graded by the KOALA software and auto-populates the radiology report with pre-defined verbiage and the Kellgren-Lawrence osteoarthritis grade to reduce dictation burden on radiologists; allowing them to focus on more nuanced opacities and lucencies of knee radiographs such as sarcomas, small avulsion fractures, 28 or subtle tibial spine fractures which may be suggestive of soft tissue pathology and may benefit from advanced cross-sectional imaging. Nehrer et al published their supporting data of KOALA ™ , which examined 124 knee radiographs randomly selected from a cohort of 4,500 radiographs across 5 institutions as part of the Osteoarthritis Initiative and compared the intraclass correlation for KOALA ™ assisted vs. unassisted reading of knee osteoarthritis. 29 For all three categories (sclerosis, osteophyte, joint space narrowing), inter-reviewer agreement between physicians in detection and Osteoarthritis Research Society International (OARSI) Scores increased after using KOALA ™ . A similar product is Radiobotics’ RBknee ™ software, which automatically generates an impression with a diagnosis of osteoarthritis along with a breakdown of the component parts contributing to the Kellgren-Lawrence osteoarthritis grade. Supporting literature is limited for RBknee ™ , though one study of 99 knee radiographs found similar Kellgren-Lawrence osteoarthritis grading between RBknee ™ product and the consensus grading between two attending radiologists. 30 Currently, there is no FDA approved x-ray evaluation of hip osteoarthritis despite similar criteria for Tönnis grading, 31 however, hip-related radiographic measurement products are on the horizon (Radiobotics’ RBhip ™ ). Quantification of spinal degenerative disease Quantitative assessment of intervertebral disc bulge in patients with degenerative spine disease can facilitate objective longitudinal monitoring of the disease. To address this issue, CoLumbo ™ supplies an AI software which quantifies the extent of disc bulge on lumbar MRI, and can help in objective surveillance of degenerative disc disease progression over years. Discussion As depicted in the main table, the majority of FDA-approved AI products are developed by companies headquartered in Europe and the North America. Another observation from this table is the higher number of neuroimaging applications compared to musculoskeletal radiology. As mentioned above, image quantification was among the most common application in the form of automated segmentation and measurement of brain volumes. However, the approval of several dose-reducing and/or time-reducing applications as well as automatic quantification and image reformatting (extracting synthetic CT information) emphasize the breadth of potential applications that AI-driven software can provide. Of note, in this review, we have not evaluated the imaging systems’ compatibility of these products. Although most software try to be as broadly applicable as possible, some AI products are only available for certain imaging technology vendors. Additionally, AI products should undergo continued assessment of their accuracy and applicability through continued learning, validation, and adjustment as the technology is adopted across different institutions and countries, and applied to different patient populations. However, plans for continued assessment or mechanisms for feedback from practicing radiologists to the companies are rarely described. In the future, more systematic evaluation and mechanisms for monitoring of AI algorithms, and reporting algorithm drift or other quality gaps would be desirable. It is important to mention that this review, we have focused on FDA-approved products – without an exhaustive review of different available AI tools worldwide. Also, products in development that do not have FDA clearance, products currently under active under review by the FDA, or products that have Conformitè Europëenne (CE) approval which allows the product to be used in clinical settings within Europe – but have not obtained FDA clearance – were not included. Conclusion The present review describes a recent and rapid increase in the number of FDA approved AI products for clinical use in the United States with applications ranging from diagnosis of ischemic stroke and intracerebral hemorrhage to quantifying the Kellgren-Lawrence grade of knee osteoarthritis. While many approved products are applied for automated quantification, there are increasingly number of approved products that move beyond quantification to detection and even diagnosis. As FDA approvals of AI technology continue to increase, it will be increasingly important for radiologists to be aware of the product landscape and play a role in the selection and application of these products in the reading room. It is likely that judicious adoption of AI will enable radiologists to provide higher quality and more efficient care for their patients. Funding: S.P. received grant support from the National Institutes of Health (K23NS118056), Doris Duke Charitable Foundation (2020097), and Foundation of American Society of Neuroradiology. Footnotes Publisher''s Disclaimer: This is a PDF file of an unedited manuscript that has been accepted for publication. As a service to our customers we are providing this early version of the manuscript. The manuscript will undergo copyediting, typesetting, and review of the resulting proof before it is published in its final form. Please note that during the production process errors may be discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the journal pertain. Conflicts of Interest: The authors and their family members have no conflicts of interest pertaining to the products mentioned within this study. Declaration of interests The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. References: 1. Yaeger KA, Martini M, Yaniv G, Oermann EK, Costa AB. United States regulatory approval of medical devices and software applications enhanced by artificial intelligence. Health Policy and Technology 2019;8:192–7. [ Google Scholar ] 2. D’hooghe MB, Gielen J, Van Remoortel A, et al. Single MRI-based volumetric assessment in clinical practice is associated with MS-related disability. Journal of Magnetic Resonance Imaging 2019;49:1312–21. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Beadnall HN, Wang C, Van Hecke W, Ribbens A, Billiet T, Barnett MH. Comparing longitudinal brain atrophy measurement techniques in a real-world multiple sclerosis clinical practice cohort: towards clinical integration? Ther Adv Neurol Disord 2019;12:1756286418823462. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Fragoso YD, Wille PR, Abreu M, et al. Correlation of clinical findings and brain volume data in multiple sclerosis. J Clin Neurosci 2017;44:155–7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Reiter K, Nielson KA, Durgerian S, et al. Five-Year Longitudinal Brain Volume Change in Healthy Elders at Genetic Risk for Alzheimer’s Disease. J Alzheimers Dis 2017;55:1363–77. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Shen Q, Loewenstein DA, Potter E, et al. Volumetric and visual rating of magnetic resonance imaging scans in the diagnosis of amnestic mild cognitive impairment and Alzheimer’s disease. Alzheimers Dement 2011;7:e101–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Major P, Décarie J-C, Nadeau A, et al. Clinical significance of isolated hippocampal volume asymmetry in childhood epilepsy. Neurology 2004;63:1503–6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Garcia-Finana M, Denby C, Keller S, Wieshmann U, Roberts N. Degree of hippocampal atrophy is related to side of 