INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5035,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11049727','The value of ultrasound in combination with 99mTc-MIBI imaging department of ultrasound medicine, for the diagnosis of ectopic parathyroid glands in the thyroid gland in patients with secondary hyperparathyroidism.','Zhao B, Chen S, Zang J, Wang X, Dai X, Liu Z, Xie P, Wang X, Wang S, Gao F, Sui X.','2024','intrathyroid etpg shpt shpt etpg etpg shpt etpg etpg etpgs etpg shpt intrathyroid shpt polyglandular shpt hyperfunction etpg etpg underdiagnosis etpg etpg etpg shpt etpg etpg shpt hitachi arietta l10 aixplorer sonic l10 clavicle preset ct670pro 140kev radiochemical 15mci radioenhancing radioenhancing w2022 shpt etpg etpg hematoxylineosin hematoxylineosin etpg heels etpg intrathyroid etpg etpg intrathyroid etpg etpg etpg etpg intrathyroid etpg intrathyroidal intrathyroidal etpg etpg etpg etpg intrathyroid etpg ectopically etpg ectasia etpg poles etpg intrathyroid etpg poles cricoid subxiphoid subperitoneal etpg parathyroids etpg poles lappas shpt shpt shpt cervico nafisi moghadam shpt misdiagnose etpgs sonographer maneuvering etpg etpg etpg intrathyroid encircling perforating gravelly etpg hypoechogenicity etpg etpg hyperparathyroid hyperparathyroid faint hyperparathyroid hyperparathyroid retroconvex radioconcentrated compensates etpg heterogeneously etpgs etpg radioactivity poles poles radioactivity shpt etpg etpg etpg shpt yulong bingxin simei bingxin simei bingxin zongjie xiaoyan jiangnan xinying xinpeng bingxin bingxin bingxin shuchang bingxin bingxin simei bingxin bingxin etpg intrathyroid shpt zf2024085 e37866 bingxin zbx1785070844 simei csm18731416446 jiangnan zangjiangnan xinying sy2007 xinpeng daixinpeng0825 zongjie liuzongjie8888 woxinfly1982 xiaoyan sy2007 shuchang sy2007 alan846829 lappas noussios anagnostis karipineni sahli somervell multigland politz intrathyroid sitges paratiroideos localizacion intratiroidea bajos intrathyroid policeni reede theurer siebolts ektopes gewebe schilddruse nebenschilddrusen pathologe lopinto parathyroidectomies quiros alioto sheu grabellus nebenschilddruse praktisches pathologischen pathologe sheu grabellus nebenschilddruse urogenitale endokrine organe gelenke skelett auflfl newyork s714 lacativa extranumerary zeze ohsato rinsho waldmann ramaswamy supernumerary intrathymic thymectomy nafisi moghadam amlelshahbaz namiranian onoda kashiwagi yabuta tsushima masuoka intrathyroidal intrathyroid e10999 hyperparathyroid micropapillary berber ballem burkhalter mah viglianti chondrogiannis bowles mohebati shaha ultrason','Abstract To investigate the value of preoperative ultrasound combined with 99m Tc-MIBI imaging for the diagnosis of ectopic intrathyroid parathyroid gland (ETPG) in patients with secondary hyperparathyroidism (SHPT). One hundred and eleven patients with SHPT who underwent total parathyroidectomy plus forearm transplantation from January 2015 to January 2022 in the Third Hospital of Hebei Medical University were selected. All patients underwent routine preoperative ultrasonography and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging, and with pathological diagnosis as the gold standard, the clinical data of ETPG patients were selected, including clinical manifestations, laboratory tests, preoperative ultrasonography and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging for localization and diagnosis, intraoperative exploration and postoperative pathology, and postoperative follow-up. To analyze the ultrasound manifestations of preoperative parathyroid hyperplasia and the results of 99m Tc-MIBI imaging in patients with ETPG. Among 111 patients with SHPT, there were 5 patients with ETPG, 1 male and 4 females with a mean age of (45.00 ± 5.05) years, and 6 ectopic parathyroid glands were located in the thyroid gland. The incidence of ETPG was 4.5% (5/111), 4 were detected by ultrasound, 2 were not detected with a diagnostic accuracy of 66.7% (4/6), 3 were positive for 99m Tc-MIBI imaging, 3 were negative with a diagnostic accuracy of 50.0% (3/6). Among them, one was not detected by ultrasound, but was positive for 99m Tc-MIBI imaging, 2 with negative 99m Tc-MIBI imaging, but all were detected by ultrasound, and one with negative 99m Tc-MIBI imaging was detected by ultrasound but misdiagnosed as a thyroid nodule. A total of 5 ETPGs were detected by ultrasound combined with 99m Tc-MIBI imaging, with a diagnostic accuracy of 83.3% (5/6). Patients’ postoperative serum calcium and serum parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels were normalized or significantly decreased from preoperative levels. Ultrasound combined with 99m Tc-MIBI imaging can achieve higher accuracy than either examination alone in the preoperative localization and diagnosis of ETPG in SHPT patients. Keywords: 99m Tc-MIBI imaging, ectopic intrathyroid parathyroid gland, ectopic parathyroid, secondary hyperparathyroidism, ultrasound 1. Introduction Secondary hyperparathyroidism (SHPT) is a condition in which hypocalcemia from various causes stimulates the parathyroid glands to proliferate and hypertrophy, causing excessive serum parathyroid hormone (PTH) secretion, which in turn leads to systemic multisystem damage, with chronic kidney disease being the most common cause, predisposing to polyglandular hyperplasia. Symptoms of early SHPT can be controlled with medication, but with prolonged dialysis, surgical intervention is eventually required. However, ectopic and extra parathyroid glands can lead to postoperative recurrence or persistent hyperfunction of the parathyroid glands, so it is important to precisely localize the lesions before surgery. The occurrence of ectopic parathyroid glands is relatively rare, and the incidence of ectopic parathyroid glands has been reported in the literature to be 8.5% to 23.0%, [ 1 , 2 ] while the occurrence of ETPG is even rarer, and the incidence of ETPG has been reported in the literature to be 0.7% to 3. 4%, [ 3 , 4 ] and both surgeons and imaging physicians have low awareness of it, especially since secondary parathyroid hyperplasia, with its much more variable number and location, is easy to cause underdiagnosis and misdiagnosis, which poses considerable challenges to diagnosis and treatment. [ 5 ] In this article, we analyzed the preoperative ultrasound manifestations of parathyroid hyperplasia and the results of 99m Tc-MIBI imaging in patients with ETPG to investigate the value of preoperative ultrasound combined with 99m Tc-MIBI imaging for the localization and diagnosis of ETPG and to improve the knowledge of ETPG. 2. Data and methods 2.1. Case selection Clinical data of patients hospitalized in our hospital who underwent total parathyroidectomy plus forearm transplantation for SHPT from January 2015 to January 2022 were collected, and a total of 111 patients were included in this study. Among them, 5 cases (4.5%) were confirmed as ETPG by intraoperative exploration and postoperative pathology, and these 5 cases of ETPG underwent parathyroid ultrasonography and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging before surgery. Patients’ PTH, alkaline phosphatase, serum calcium, and serum phosphorus biochemistry were also recorded. 2.2. Inclusion and exclusion criteria Inclusion criteria: patients with chronic kidney disease undergoing regular dialysis with a clear diagnosis of SHPT; at least one enlarged parathyroid gland detected by ultrasonography and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging; total parathyroidectomy plus forearm transplantation; and postoperative pathology confirming parathyroid hyperplasia or adenoma or carcinoma. Exclusion criteria: patients with primary hyperparathyroidism; patients with severe coagulation disorders; patients with concomitant severe cardiac, pulmonary, cerebral dysfunction, and other systemic diseases who cannot tolerate surgery; patients with a history of renal transplantation; patients who did not undergo preoperative ultrasonography of the parathyroid glands and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging. 2.3. Ultrasound examination A Hitachi ARIETTA 70 ultrasound machine with an L10-12 line array probe at 10-12 MHz or an Aixplorer French Sonic ultrasound machine with an L10-2 line array probe at 2 to 10 MHz were used. In the supine position, the thyroid region of the neck is fully exposed, up to the angle of the mandible, down to the clavicle, and up to the internal jugular vein on both sides. Apply a coupling agent to the detection site and apply the preset conditions of the device to comprehensively scan between the dorsal posterior border of the thyroid gland and the long neck muscle, and between the trachea and the common carotid artery to check for abnormal echoes, and after detecting suspected parathyroid lesions, record the lesion location, number, size, morphology, borders, internal echoes, and color blood supply. 2.4. 99m Tc-MIBI dual time-phase imaging methods The imaging device was a GE Discovery NM/CT670pro single photon emission tomography (SPECT/CT) scanner with a low-energy, high-resolution collimator, a 128 × 128 acquisition matrix, an energy peak setting of 140keV, a 20% acquisition window width, a magnification of 2.57, and an acquisition time of 10 minutes per image. No special preparation was required before the examination, and patients were placed in the supine position with the field of view from the neck to the upper chest. The 99m Tc-MIBI imaging agent (provided by Atomic High-Tech Co., Ltd., radiochemical purity > 95%) was injected intravenously with 15mCi, and the static image of the anterior neck was obtained in the early phase (15 minutes) and the delayed phase (120 minutes). The image was reconstructed by computed tomography and the 99m Tc-MIBI biphasic image was obtained. 2.5. Analysis of conventional ultrasound and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging images Ultrasound images were analyzed by 2 experienced senior sonographers. Ultrasound of the parathyroid glands showed hypoechoic nodules with regular or irregular morphology, oval shape, clear borders, and detectable internal and peripheral blood flow signals. The 99m Tc-MIBI images were jointly analyzed by 2 experienced senior nuclear medicine physicians. Positive diagnostic criteria were: abnormal radioenhancing shadows in the neck or upper chest in the early phase and residual radioenhancing shadows in the delayed phase. 2.6. Parathyroidectomy and pathologic diagnosis The patient underwent total parathyroidectomy with forearm grafting under general anesthesia and rapid frozen specimens for pathologic diagnosis. 2.7. Ethical statement This study has been approved by the ethical review of the Third Hospital of Hebei Medical University [Ethical Acceptance No. W2022-003-1]. Informed consent was obtained from participants for all patient data. 3. Results 3.1. Clinical data There were 111 patients with SHPT who underwent surgical treatment in our hospital from January 2015 to January 2022. Intraoperative exploration and postoperative pathology confirmed that there were 5 cases of ETPG (Fig. 1 ), of which 1 was male and 4 were female, with a mean age of (45.00 ± 5.05) years. A total of 21 parathyroid glands were explored during surgery, of which 6 were ectopic to the thyroid gland and 15 were in the normal parathyroid position. The clinical data of the 5 patients with ETPG are shown in Table 1 , and the laboratory test data are shown in Table 2 . Figure 1. Open in a new tab (A) Ectopic parathyroid gland pathology image: The image on the left shows the thyroid tissue. The image on the right shows ectopic parathyroid tissue (hematoxylineosin [H&E], original magnification 100). (B) Ectopic parathyroid gland pathology image: The image on the left shows the thyroid tissue. The image on the right shows ectopic parathyroid tissue (hematoxylineosin [H&E], original magnification 100). Table 1. Clinical data of 5 patients with ETPG. Case Gender Age (yr) Main point of view (of a case) 1 Male 43 Hemodialysis for 8 yr, elevated PTH for 3 yr with bone pain 2 Female 42 Hemodialysis for 9 mo pain in both heels and knees, noticeable after activity 3 Female 43 Hemodialysis for 12 yr and poor internal fistula function for 2 d 4 Female 54 Hemodialysis for 7 yr, elevated PTH for 2 yr 5 Female 43 Hemodialysis for 4 yr, elevated PTH for 4 mo, pain in both lower limbs and knee joints with itching of skin Open in a new tab ETPG = ectopic intrathyroid parathyroid gland, PTH = parathyroid hormone. Table 2. Laboratory test data of 5 ETPG patients. Case Preoperative PTH (pg/mL) Preoperative serum calcium (mmol/L) Preoperative serum phosphorus (mmol/L) Preoperative ALP (U/L) Postoperative PTH (pg/mL) Postoperative serum calcium (mmol/L) Postoperative serum phosphorus (mmol/L) 1 1563.70 2.40 2.05 437 27.01 1.59 1.06 2 1927.40 2.57 2.04 562 7.00 2.12 1.85 3 2965.40 2.63 2.25 1207 25.70 1.96 1.78 4 3031.30 2.91 1.65 893 9.80 2.42 1.39 5 1538.20 2.43 2.08 476 34.46 1.79 1.37 Open in a new tab ALP = alkaline phosphatase, ETPG = ectopic intrathyroid parathyroid gland, PTH = parathyroid hormone. 3.2. Preoperative imaging examination Preoperative parathyroid ultrasonography and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging were performed in all cases, and the number, location, size, and presence or absence of ectopic parathyroid glands were clarified by preoperative localization. The comparison of preoperative ultrasonography and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging results of the 5 patients with ETPG is shown in Table 3 . Table 3. Comparison of preoperative ultrasonography and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging results in 5 ETPG patients. Case Display number of lesions Location of parathyroid lesions detected by ultrasound 99m Tc-MIBI imaging of the location of parathyroid lesions LU LL RU RL ITT LU LL RU RL ITT 1 5 + + − + − + + + + + 2 4 + + + + − + + + − − 3 4 + + + + − + + − 4 4 + + ++ − − ++ 5 4 + + + + + − + − Open in a new tab − = negative, + = positive, ++ = the ETPG has 2, ETPG = ectopic intrathyroid parathyroid gland, ITT = inside the thyroid, LL = left lower, LU = left upper, RL = right lower, RU = right upper. Preoperative ultrasound combined with 99m Tc-MIBI imaging showed a total of 21 parathyroid foci in the 5 ETPG patients shown in Table 3 , of which 19 were detected by ultrasound for a positive diagnostic rate of 90.5% (19/21) and a total of 14 by 99m Tc-MIBI imaging for a positive diagnostic rate of 66.7% (14/21), with ultrasound showing 4 intrathyroidal ectopic parathyroid foci. 99m Tc-MIBI imaging showed 3 intrathyroidal ectopic parathyroid foci. 3.3. Postoperative pathology All 111 patients underwent total parathyroidectomy with forearm transplantation, and 5 patients were confirmed to have ETPG by intraoperative exploration and postoperative pathology, and a comparison of intraoperative exploration and postoperative pathology results is shown in Table 4 . Table 4. Comparison of intraoperative exploration and postoperative pathologic findings in 5 ETPG patients. Case Number of lesions Intraoperative exploration of the location of parathyroid lesions Postoperative pathology (nodular hyperplasia of parathyroid glands) LU LL RU RL ITT LU LL RU RL ITT 1 4 + + + + + + + + 2 5 + + + + + + + + + + 3 4 + + + + + + + + 4 4 + + ++ + + ++ 5 4 + + + + + + + + Open in a new tab + = positive; the ETPG has 2, ETPG = ectopic intrathyroid parathyroid gland, ITT = inside the thyroid, LL = left lower, LU = left upper, RL = right lower, RU = right upper A total of 21 parathyroid lesions were detected intraoperatively in the 5 ETPG patients shown in Table 4 , and postoperative pathology showed that all of them were nodular hyperplasia of the parathyroid glands, of which 6 were ectopically located in the thyroid gland. 3.4. Comparison of the specific sites of ETPG ectasia with the results of ultrasonography and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging in 5 cases is shown in Table 5 Table 5. Five cases of ETPG ectopic site with ultrasonography and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging results. Case Ectopic site Size (cm) Ultrasonic examination 99m Tc-MIBI 1 Right middle thyroid gland 1.5 × 1.5 Undetected Positive 2 Middle and lower pole of the left thyroid gland 1.0 × 1.0 Detected and misdiagnosed as a thyroid nodule Negative 3 Upper middle pole of the right thyroid gland 0.5 × 0.5 Detected Negative 4 Left and right inferior poles of the thyroid gland Left lower 2.0 × 1.0, right lower 1.0 × 1.0 Detected Positive 5 Left upper pole of the thyroid gland 1.2 × 1.1 Detected Negative Open in a new tab ETPG = ectopic intrathyroid parathyroid gland. As shown in Table 5 , there were 6 ectopic parathyroid glands in the thyroid gland, and 4 were detected by ultrasound, with a diagnostic accuracy of 66.7% (4/6), and 3 positive and 3 negative 99m Tc-MIBI imaging, with a diagnostic accuracy of 50.0% (3/6). 3.5. Postoperative follow-up The serum calcium and PTH of the 5 ETPG patients returned to normal or decreased significantly compared to the preoperative period, and the symptoms of bone pain and skin itching were reduced to varying degrees, with no postoperative complications or recurrences. 4. Discussion The parathyroid glands are usually located on the dorsal surface of the upper and lower poles of the thyroid gland; the upper parathyroid glands are usually medial to the back of the lateral lobes of the thyroid at the level of the cricoid cartilage, and the lower parathyroid glands are lateral to the back of the lateral lobes of the thyroid at the level of the subxiphoid artery near the level of the recurrent laryngeal nerve. Parathyroid glands during embryonic development, the inferior parathyroid glands can migrate to various anatomical locations, such as the superior mediastinum, carotid sheath, thymus, and even the thyroid gland. [ 6 , 7 ] In a review of 56 autopsies performed at the Federal University of São Paulo, Brazil, at least one ectopic parathyroid gland was found in 42.8% of cases, and the ectopic sites were predominantly in the mediastinum and thymus (19.6%), the subperitoneal space of the thyroid (12.5%), and the parenchyma of the thyroid (5.4%). [ 8 ] The incidence of ETPG has been reported in the literature to range from 0.7% to 3.4%, and 70.6% occur in the right lobe of the thyroid gland, with a prevalence in the lower extreme 1/3. [ 3 , 4 ] Six parathyroids were ectopic in the thyroid gland in the 5 patients with ETPG in this study, including one in the upper pole of the left thyroid, one in the lower pole of the left thyroid, one in the middle and lower poles of the left thyroid, one in the middle and upper pole of the right thyroid, one in the lower pole of the right thyroid, and one in the middle part of the right thyroid, which may be related to the rarity of the disease and the small sample size of the present study. In most cases, the body has 2 pairs of parathyroid glands, but the number is not fixed, with between 2.0% and 15.0% of cases describing between 2 and 6 parathyroid glands. Some authors have reported up to 12 parathyroid glands in their studies. [ 9 – 11 ] In a large study of 942 patients, Lappas et al (2012) reported 3 parathyroid glands in 2% (19/942) and 5 parathyroid glands in 5% (47/942) of patients. [ 1 ] In our group of 111 patients with SHPT, 1 patient had 5 parathyroid glands and the 5th parathyroid gland was located in the thyroid gland. Studies of patients with SHPT have reported a prevalence of ectopic parathyroid glands of 35.1-45.7%. [ 12 – 14 ] Therefore, in patients with SHPT, even if 4 parathyroid glands are identified and removed, there is still a risk of persistent or recurrent hyperparathyroidism due to the presence of ectopic and additional glands. Accurate preoperative identification of all parathyroid glands is critical to avoid secondary surgery. At present, the technical means for preoperative localization and diagnosis of parathyroid surgery at home and abroad mainly include ultrasonography and cervico-thoracic 99m Tc-MIBI imaging. Nafisi Moghadam et al (2017) reported that neck ultrasonography has a diagnostic sensitivity of 80.0% for parathyroid lesions. [ 15 ] Noda et al (2014) reported that 99m Tc-MIBI had a diagnostic accuracy of 88.5% for parathyroid lesions, and that combining it with ultrasound increased localization accuracy [ 16 ] All patients in this group underwent preoperative parathyroid ultrasonography and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging. Based on these 111 cases of SHPT, the authors concluded that the ultrasonographic manifestations of secondary parathyroid hyperplasia are often characterized by bilateral hypoechoic parathyroid nodules with regular or irregular morphology, oval shape, with clear borders, uniform or uneven internal echoes, a small number of internal dark areas of fluid, internal gross calcifications or peripheral annular calcifications, and abundant blood flow signals around the periphery of the nodule and inside the nodule. In general, the ultrasonographic features of normally located hyperplastic parathyroid glands are generally more prominent and easier to distinguish from thyroid nodules and peripheral lymph nodes, and experienced sonographers usually rarely misdiagnose or miss the diagnosis. However, ectopic parathyroid glands, especially ETPGs, are easily misdiagnosed and missed due to the low level of sonographer awareness and the reliance on operator maneuvering and experience, especially in the presence of concomitant thyroid nodules, ETPG is more likely to be misdiagnosed as a thyroid nodule. In the 5 cases of ETPG in this study, 1 case was not detected by preoperative ultrasonography, 1 case was detected but misdiagnosed as a thyroid nodule, and 3 cases were detected, for a total of 4. The accuracy of ultrasonographic diagnosis of ETPG was 66.7% (4/6), and ultrasonography showed mostly hypoechoic nodules in the thyroid gland, with regular or irregular morphology, clear borders, homogeneous or inhomogeneous internal echogenicity, and coarse calcification in some of the internal parts, and abundant blood flow signals were visible in the periphery and interior (Figs. 2 and 3 ), which was consistent with the sonographic presentation of secondary parathyroid hyperplasia in normal anatomic locations. Figure 2. Open in a new tab (A)Cross-sectional image of the thyroid gland: Ectopic parathyroid glands visible in the right lobe of the thyroid. (B) Cross-sectional image of the thyroid gland:Ectopic parathyroid glands visible in the left lobe of the thyroid. Figure 3. Open in a new tab (A) Longitudinal image of the thyroid gland: The arrow points to the parathyroid gland, which is located within the thyroid gland. (B) Longitudinal image of the thyroid gland: Ectopic intrathyroid parathyroid blood flow diagram. Ultrasound lesions of parathyroid hyperplasia often present as hypoechoic nodules with well-defined borders, regular or irregular morphology, oval shape, coarse calcifications and ring calcifications are more common, and blood flow is more abundant and encircling or perforating blood flow is seen; benign thyroid nodules often present as anechoic or hyperechoic nodules with well-defined borders, regular morphology, visible coarse calcifications, non-central type of blood supply, and visible blood flow signal around the nodule; malignant thyroid nodules often show a centralized type of blood supply with abundant blood flow, and blood flow signals can be seen within and around the nodule, but they often show hypoechoic or very hypoechoic nodules with unclear borders, irregular morphology, and a longitudinal to transverse ratio of often > 1, and gravelly calcifications are common within. Characteristic hyperechoic lines at the ventral junction of ETPG and thyroid have been reported in the literature, which is different from halo-like hypoechogenicity around thyroid nodules. [ 17 , 18 ] However, in practice, it is still difficult to distinguish ETPG from thyroid nodules, and it is necessary to combine it with other tests, such as ultrasound shear wave elastography and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging. Meng et al (2018) reported that ultrasound shear wave elastography could increase the detection rate of parathyroid lesions to a certain extent, [ 19 ] providing a reference basis for the localization and diagnosis of preoperative ETPG. 99m Tc-MIBI is a commonly used parathyroid localization imaging method, and its main imaging mechanism is that both normal thyroid tissue and hyperparathyroid tissue can take up 99m Tc-MIBI to different degrees, but hyperparathyroid tissue is rich in mitochondria and neovascularization, and the ratio of MIBI uptake is greater than that of thyroid tissue, and the elution rate is slower, so the early phase (15-30 minutes after 99m Tc-MIBI injection), mainly a faint shadow of the thyroid gland was seen, and the hyperparathyroid glands were obvious, and in the delayed phase (120–180 minutes after 99m Tc-MIBI injection), the normal thyroid tissue was basically faded, and the hyperparathyroid foci were more clearly visualized. [ 20 , 21 ] Therefore, this examination can provide higher quality and more accurate localization of hyperparathyroidism. Some researchers demonstrated that 99m Tc-MIBI imaging had a high sensitivity and specificity to locate parathyroid hyperplasia lesions or multiple lesions. [ 22 – 26 ] However, small size of parathyroid glands or cystic degeneration may cause false negative results. Chen et al (2017) [ 27 ] found that 99m Tc-MIBI imaging often fails to visualize microscopic parathyroid glands with low metabolism and a maximum diameter of < 10 mm, which has a significant rate of missed diagnosis. Inflammatory cervical lymph nodes or retroconvex thyroid nodules may also appear as radioconcentrated foci, making them prone to false positives, [ 28 ] and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging is unable to clarify the anatomical relationship between foci and surrounding tissues, whereas conventional ultrasound can clearly visualize the parathyroid glands with a maximum diameter of 3-5 mm and their blood flow distribution characteristics, and can also clearly visualize the cystic foci and the anatomical relationship with the surrounding tissues, which compensates for the deficiencies of 99m Tc-MIBI imaging. Zhang et al (2006) and Liu et al (2004) [ 29 , 30 ] indicated that the detection rate of ectopic parathyroid lesions by ultrasound combined with 99m Tc-MIBI dual time-phase imaging was significantly higher than that of any single examination method, such as ultrasound, CT and MRI. In this study, 5 patients with ETPG had a total of 21 parathyroid glands, and 99m Tc-MIBI imaging showed 14 (Fig. 4 A), of which one was a false positive, for a diagnostic accuracy of 61.9% (13/21), and ultrasound detected 19 of them for a diagnostic accuracy of 90.5% (19/21). A total of 6 parathyroid glands were heterogeneously located in the thyroid gland, with 3 positive and 3 negative 99m Tc-MIBI imaging, with a diagnostic accuracy of 50.0% (3/6), and 4 detected by ultrasound and 2 undetected, with a diagnostic accuracy of 66.7% (4/6). Among them, 2 with negative 99m Tc-MIBI imaging, but both were detected by ultrasound, one not detected by ultrasound with positive 99m Tc-MIBI imaging (Fig. 4 B), and one with negative 99m Tc-MIBI imaging was detected by ultrasound but misdiagnosed as a thyroid nodule. A total of 5 ETPGs were detected by ultrasound combined with 99m Tc-MIBI imaging, with a diagnostic accuracy of 83.3% (5/6). In our study, ultrasound combined with 99m Tc-MIBI imaging significantly improved the diagnostic accuracy of ETPG, which is consistent with the literature. Figure 4. Open in a new tab (A) 99m Tc-MIBI imaging of parathyroid gland: 15-min and 120-min 99m Tc-MIBI images showed areas of increased radioactivity at the upper and lower poles of the left thyroid lobe and at the upper and lower poles of the right thyroid lobe. (B) 99m Tc-MIBI imaging of ectopic parathyroid: 15-min and 120-min 99m Tc-MIBI images showed area of increased radioactivity in the right middle lobe of the thyroid. Of the 111 patients with SHPT in this study, 5 patients (4.5%) had ectopic parathyroid glands. Four of them underwent a single complete parathyroidectomy and one underwent a secondary surgery for ectopic parathyroidectomy. The variation of parathyroid position increases the difficulty of preoperative examination and clinical treatment, and the number and position of secondary parathyroid hyperplasia are more variable, which makes the requirements of imaging physicians more stringent, and they need to be more vigilant in preoperative localization and diagnosis, and they need to be proficient in mastering the common position of parathyroid glands and common ectopic anatomical positions, Combined with the patient medical history, ultrasound examination combined with 99m Tc-MIBI imaging, so as to increase the detection rate, to achieve the minimization of leakage of diagnosis, to improve the diagnostic accuracy, to avoid the harm to patients caused by secondary surgery, and to improve their prognosis. Because of the clinical rarity of ETPG disease, the sample size included in this study was small, and because of the limitations of the retrospective design, which may affect the accuracy of the study, further multicenter large-sample studies are needed in the future to validate the diagnostic value of ultrasound combined with 99m Tc-MIBI imaging for ETPG. In conclusion, ultrasound combined with 99m Tc-MIBI imaging can achieve higher accuracy than either examination alone in the preoperative localization and diagnosis of ETPG in SHPT patients. Acknowledgments The authors thank surgeon Yulong Liang for his contribution to this study. Author contributions Conceptualization: Bingxin Zhao, Simei Chen. Data curation: Bingxin Zhao, Simei Chen. Formal analysis: Bingxin Zhao, Zongjie Liu, Xiaoyan Wang. Funding acquisition: Xin Sui. Investigation: Jiangnan Zang, Xinying Wang, Xinpeng Dai. Methodology: Bingxin Zhao. Project administration: Bingxin Zhao, Xin Sui. Resources: Bingxin Zhao, Peng Xie, Shuchang Wang, Feng Gao. Supervision: Bingxin Zhao. Visualization: Bingxin Zhao, Simei Chen. Writing – original draft: Bingxin Zhao. Writing – review & editing: Bingxin Zhao, Xin Sui. Abbreviations: ETPG ectopic intrathyroid parathyroid gland PTH Parathyroid hormone SHPT secondary hyperparathyroidism This work was supported by Department of Finance of Hebei (Project No. ZF2024085) and Health Commission of Hebei Province (Project No. 20241065). The authors have no conflicts of interest to disclose. All data generated or analyzed during this study are included in this published article [and its supplementary information files]. How to cite this article: Zhao B, Chen S, Zang J, Wang X, Dai X, Liu Z, Xie P, Wang X, Wang S, Gao F, Sui X. The value of ultrasound in combination with 99m Tc-MIBI imaging department of ultrasound medicine, for the diagnosis of ectopic parathyroid glands in the thyroid gland in patients with secondary hyperparathyroidism. Medicine 2024;103:17(e37866). Contributor Information Bingxin Zhao, Email: zbx1785070844@163.com. Simei Chen, Email: csm18731416446@163.com. Jiangnan Zang, Email: zangjiangnan@126.com. Xinying Wang, Email: wang-sy2007@163.com. Xinpeng Dai, Email: daixinpeng0825@163.com. Zongjie Liu, Email: liuzongjie8888@163.com. Peng Xie, Email: woxinfly1982@126.com. Xiaoyan Wang, Email: wang-sy2007@163.com. Shuchang Wang, Email: wang-sy2007@163.com. Feng Gao, Email: alan846829@163.com. References [1]. Lappas D, Noussios G, Anagnostis P, et al. Location, number and morphology of parathyroid glands: results from a large anatomical series. Anat Sci Int. 2012;87:160–4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [2]. Karipineni F, Sahli Z, Somervell H, et al. Are preoperative sestamibi scans useful for identifying ectopic parathyroid glands in patients with expected multigland parathyroid disease? Surgery. 2018;163:35–41. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [3]. Goodman A, Politz D, Lopez J, et al. Intrathyroid parathyroid adenoma: incidence and location--the case against thyroid lobectomy. Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg. 2011;144:867–71. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [4]. Ros S, Sitges-Serra A, Pereira JA, et al. Adenomas paratiroideos de localiz