INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5043,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10409879','Deep learning applications in osteoarthritis imaging.','Kijowski R, Fritz J, Deniz CM.','2023','composing handcrafted kernels rectified replaces downsample computations rudimentary softmax reuses splits hyperparameters fastmri fastmri fastmri fastmri agingresearchbiobank search_term mrnet stanfordmlgroup competitions mrnet iwoai kellgren dess ryai ski10 ski10 undersampling euclidean vgg vgg holdout pedoia patellar patellar fullthickness kellgren osteophyte oarsi osteophyte antony vgg wndchrm tiulpin siamese multiclass swiecicki concatenate tiulpin squeeze oarsi osteophyte scroll stacks segmenting prasoon segmenting segnet segmenting spoiled spgr spgr patella patellar quadriceps patellar infrapatellar popliteal ambellan segmenting spgr dess spgr dess segmenting menisci spgr dess spgr dess gaj segmenting dess segmenting discriminator segmenting spgr dess spgr amroa amroa panfilov vgg19 segmenting dess patella subregional vgg19 segmenting spgr dess spgr dess dess spgr segmenting dess segmenting vgg pedoia spgr segnet assd voe segnet ambellan spgr assd dess voe assd voe spgr dess gaj dess unet assd spgr panfilov dess spgr dess schiratti efficientnet tolpadi dess densenet dess siamese densenet spinecho spgr spoiled recalledecho dess assd voe hoffa tiulpin densenet concatenated schiratti efficientnet fivefold sevenfold tolpadi densenet dess dess sevenfold densenet womac siamese siamese tenfold densenet projector pedoia densenet relaxometry relaxometry translatability segmenting segmenting accomplishes ensembling translatability korfiatis akkus binvignat pedoia butte louati klatzmann berenbaum e001998 lecun bedson chea mandell goodfellow courville deeplearningbook quatman hettrich spindler menashe hirko losina kloppenburg samsonov blankenbaker larison kanarek pedoia mehany bucknor majumdar pfj astuto flament namiri bharadwaj e200165 kellgren osteoarthrosis mazzuca almazor renner oarsi sheehy culham culvenor engen oiestad engebretsen risberg kellgren oarsi arthrosc antony icpr pedoia noworolski majumdar tiulpin thevenot rahtu lehenkari saarakkala kidzinski halilaj venkataraman oei ehg e190065 swiecicki mather jcm9103341 tiulpin saarakkala diagnostics10110932 yushkevich piven hazlett fripp crozier warfield ourselin cartilages tamez schreyer totterman prasoon igel lauze triplanar samsonov kijowski deformable kijowski ambellan tack ehlke zachow pedoia majumdar relaxometry gaj colak chundru gaj nanavati henson segmenting panfilov tiulpin nieminen saarakkala casula subregional eckstein kemnitz konukoglu fuerst femorotibial magma eckstein chaudhari fuerst gaisberger kemnitz caliva iriondo mortazi jambawalikar bagci e200078 astrup bliddal osteoarthr cartil oars osteoarthr messier loeser rejeski sevick astrup bliddal ettinger messier applegate rejeski roddy roddy arden birrell brosseau macleay tugwell cd004259 cd004259 deyle matekel ryder fransen fransen crosbie edmonds deyle felson eckstein burstein totterman tamez dabrowski conaghan peterfy guermazi woodworth osteoarthr cartil oars osteoarthr a112 reichmann maillefert conaghan losina osteoarthr cartil raynauld dmoad qvist pastoureau karsdal dmoad hellio graverand gastineau osteoarthr cartil oars osteoarthr hellio graverand gastineau osteoarthr cartil nevitt losina felson sack aliabadi nevitt leyland javaid kiran ghodadra winalski miniaci osteoarthr cartil tanamas cicuttini wluka urquhart malalignment losina nevitt roemer guermazi roemer kwoh hannon jakicic boudreau patellofemoral tibiofemoral roemer guermazi felson nevitt crema ayral woodworth mackillop tibiofemoral osteoarthr cartil oars osteoarthr atukorala kwoh guermazi roemer boudreau hannon crema guermazi roemer tibiofemoral osteoarthr cartil oars osteoarthr moisio chmiel eckstein guermazi almagor subregional wluka tuck wluka tuck cicuttini dore winzenberg r222 scher osteoarthrosis antony infrapatellar tiulpin bierma zeinstra thevenot rahtu meurs haj mirzaian demehri samsonov neogi schiratti herent cahane dachary clozel geras babb tolpadi pedoia majumdar deniz mizaian demehri samsonov guermazi felson guermazi capellini kolachalama siamese majumdar pedoia osteoarthr pedoia majumdar osteoarthr cartil canu isbi','Abstract Deep learning (DL) is one of the most exciting new areas in medical imaging. This article will provide a review of current applications of DL in osteoarthritis (OA) imaging, including methods used for cartilage lesion detection, OA diagnosis, cartilage segmentation, and OA risk assessment. DL techniques have been shown to have similar diagnostic performance as human readers for detecting and grading cartilage lesions within the knee on MRI. A variety of DL methods have been developed for detecting and grading the severity of knee OA and various features of knee OA on X-rays using standardized classification systems with diagnostic performance similar to human readers. Multiple DL approaches have been described for fully automated segmentation of cartilage and other knee tissues and have achieved higher segmentation accuracy than currently used methods with substantial reductions in segmentation times. Various DL models analyzing baseline X-rays and MRI have been developed for OA risk assessment. These models have shown high diagnostic performance for predicting a wide variety of OA outcomes, including the incidence and progression of radiographic knee OA, the presence and progression of knee pain, and future total knee replacement. The preliminary results of DL applications in OA imaging have been encouraging. However, many DL techniques require further technical refinement to maximize diagnostic performance. Furthermore, the generalizability of DL approaches needs to be further investigated in prospective studies using large image datasets acquired at different institutions with different imaging hardware before they can be implemented in clinical practice and research studies. Keywords: Deep learning, Artificial intelligence, Osteoarthritis, Knee, MRI, X-rays Introduction Artificial intelligence refers to intelligence demonstrated by a machine that performs tasks that typically require human knowledge in a manner that resembles how humans solve problems. Machine learning is a branch of artificial intelligence that aims to automatically extract patterns or features out of data [ 1 ]. Machine learning techniques have been used for decades in the field of osteoarthritis (OA) imaging, with their numerous applications summarized in a recent review article [ 2 ]. Deep learning (DL), a special class of machine learning, is a more advanced form of artificial intelligence that uses multiple levels of representation obtained by composing simple modules that each transforms the representation at one level into a representation at a higher, slightly more abstract level. With the combination of enough such transformations, complex functions can be learned. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) are a notable subclass of DL algorithms that have dominated the fields of computer vision and medical imaging and have surpassed human abilities in many important tasks. Unlike classic machine learning methods, CNNs do not require carefully handcrafted features and can automatically learn a representative subset of features given enough training data [ 3 ]. This article will review current DL applications in OA imaging including methods used for cartilage lesion detection, OA diagnosis, cartilage segmentation, and OA risk assessment. The article will focus on the knee as it is the joint most commonly affected by OA [ 4 ] and by far the most frequent joint evaluated with DL applications in musculoskeletal and OA imaging [ 5 ]. Overview of deep learning The CNN is the main neural network architecture used in medical imaging. A CNN consists of a series of convolutional layers interspersed with a series of pooling layers. Each convolution layer consists of one or more filters or kernels, which analyze features of regional voxels in the input image to generate feature maps. Units of the feature maps are connected to the units of feature maps in other convolutional layers by filter weights. An activation function, typically a rectified linear unit, replaces all negative values in the feature map with zero, which allows only pertinent features to pass from one layer to the next. The pooling layer is then used to downsample the feature maps and merge similar features by using an arithmetic operation, such as taking the maximum value (max pooling) or average value (average pooling) of adjacent units in the feature map. This pooling process reduces the number of parameters and computations in the neural network and makes the network less sensitive to distortions and translations in the input image [ 3 ]. The CNN connects multiple convolution layers in a deep network that can detect a hierarchy of features that increasingly become more abstract and complex. For example, the filters in the first few layers may be used to identify intensity variations, edges, and patterns in the input image at a rudimentary level. Next, new sets of filters at intermediate-level layers can then process the output from low-level features to construct composite information such as local shapes and patterns. This process repeats and continues until a high-level layer, where multiple global features are combined to represent a unique set of patterns in the image. The multiple convolution layers of the CNN are then connected to a fully connected layer. This output layer of the CNN uses a softmax activation function to combine the features extracted from the different convolution layers in order to classify the input image into different categories [ 3 ]. DL applications in medical imaging have primarily used supervised learning. In supervised learning, the DL model requires labels for each sample such that labels and samples are used concurrently for model training and testing. DL approaches will generally achieve acceptable diagnostic performance for classification tasks when approximately 5000 sample-label pairs are used for supervised model training [ 6 ]. However, acquiring such a large number of label-sample pairs is time-consuming and labor-intensive. For this reason, technical advancements in DL have begun to move the field toward semi-supervised and unsupervised learning. In semi-supervised learning, a combination of available label-sample pairs and unlabeled samples are used to improve DL model accuracy and generalizability. In unsupervised learning, unlabeled samples are used to train a DL model with a small number of label-sample pairs using transfer learning. Transfer learning is a method that reuses knowledge gained by pre-trained models as a starting point for a new task. Transfer learning improves model performance compared to a model trained without knowledge transfer and reduces the time required for model training [ 3 ]. DL models are developed using a training dataset and evaluated on an independent, hold-out testing dataset. These datasets need to be identified randomly using subject-level splits to prevent information bias from the training to the testing phase. During model training, a subset of the training dataset can be used as a validation dataset with a typical 80 to 20% split for the training and validation datasets, respectively. The validation dataset is used for parameter tuning and optimal model selection during the training process. Alternatively, k-fold cross-validation can be used to optimize hyperparameters and identify the best performing model. Model evaluation on the hold-out testing dataset identifies its true performance and generalizability [ 3 ]. External testing datasets are useful to further assess model generalizability by evaluating model performance on a subject population distinctly different from the one used for model training and validation. Table 1 summarizes the external image datasets that are currently available for model training and testing for a variety of DL applications in osteoarthritis imaging. Table 1. Publically available external training and testing datasets for DL applications in osteoarthritis imaging Dataset Application Description Link FastMRI Reconstruction Dataset containing knee MRI scans provided to investigate deep learning acceleration https://fastmri.org FastMRI + Classification Dataset containing clinical knee pathology annotations for the fastMRI dataset https://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.03812.pdf OAI Classification Dataset containing clinical data, patient-reported outcomes, biospecimen analyses, quantitative image analyses, radiographs, and magnetic resonance images on subjects with or at risk for knee osteoarthritis https://nda.nih.gov/oai/ MOST Classification Dataset containing clinical data, patient-reported outcomes, biospecimen analyses, quantitative image analyses, radiographs, and magnetic resonance images on subjects with or at risk for knee osteoarthritis https://agingresearchbiobank.nia.nih.gov/studies/most/?search_term=most MRnet Classification Dataset containing 1104 (80.6%) abnormal exams, with 319 (23.3%) ACL tears and 508 (37.1%) meniscal tears; labels were obtained through manual extraction from clinical reports https://stanfordmlgroup.github.io/competitions/mrnet/ IWOAI Segmentation Dataset containing 88 patients with Kellgren-Lawrence osteoarthritis grades 1–4 who underwent scanning at two time points (baseline and 1 year), resulting in 176 three-dimensional (3D) double-echo steady-state (DESS) volumes https://pubs.rsna.org/doi/full/10.1148/ryai.2021200078 SKI10 Segmentation Dataset containing cartilage and bone segmentations from 150 knee MRI volumes https://ski10.grand-challenge.org Open in a new tab DL models contain a large number of weights to be identified during training. This necessitates the use of a large number of samples to prevent overfitting the training data. Overfitting results in poor generalization of the DL model, which impedes the use of models with high accuracy on the hold-out testing dataset. When selecting the number of layers and weights of the DL model, the user needs to first identify if the DL model has enough capacity to learn and then apply techniques to prevent overfitting. Overfitting can be prevented by training the model with more data or using an early stopping criterion. The amount of new training data that needs to be collected and labeled can be determined by training a DL model with a different fraction of training data and observing the objective metrics. Usually, the amount of additional data versus the improvement of objective metrics reach a plateau, which can be used as a criterion to decide on the number of additional samples and the expected objective metrics for the task [ 3 ]. In general, datasets in the medical domain contain an unbalanced number of classes. For example, the number of cases without a disease is typically larger than the number of cases with a disease, or the segmentation volume of a region of interest covers only a small region within the image. Training DL models in conventional approaches tend to result in prediction errors with a bias toward the group with a larger number. To overcome this challenge, various algorithmic techniques have been developed such as oversampling from the underrepresented class, undersampling from the overrepresented class, hybrid approaches, generating synthetic samples, and weighting the loss function based on class frequency. Model evaluation in unbalanced datasets also needs to be considered. For example, performance metrics such as accuracy, area under the receiver operating characteristics curve (AUC), and area under the precision-recall curve are commonly used to compare different DL models by quantifying how well the models distinguishes between classes when trained and tested on a balanced dataset. However, such performance metrics could be misleading for an unbalanced dataset. Performance measures such as confusion matrix, precision, recall, F1-score, and Matthews correlation coefficient can provide more insight into the diagnostic performance of DL model trained on an unbalanced dataset. [ 3 ]. Table 2 summarizes the commonly used metrics to evaluate DL model performance for classification and segmentation applications in osteoarthritis imaging. Table 2. Commonly used methods to evaluate deep learning model performance for classification and segmentation applications in osteoarthritis imaging Metric Description Definition Meaning Classification Accuracy Percentage of correct classifications of model ( T P + T N ) / ( P + N ) Higher is better Precision Ratio of correct positive classifications to all positive classifications of model T P / ( T P + F P ) Higher is better Recall/sensitivity Ratio of correct positive classifications of model to all reference standard positive classifications T P / ( T P + F N ) Higher is better F1-score Harmonic mean of model precision and recall 2(Recall*Precision) / (Recall + Precision) Higher is better Matthews correlation coefficient Pearson correlation coefficient between model and reference standard classifications N/A Ranges from — 1 to 1 with — 1 indicating worse and + 1 best agreement Area under receiver operating characteristics curve Aggregate measure of model performance across all possible classification thresholds N/A 0.5–0.6 = unsatisfactory 0.6–0.7 = satisfactory 0.7–0.8 = good 0.8–0.9 = very good 0.9–1 = excellent Kappa coefficient Measure of agreement between model and reference standard classifications 2 ( T P * T N − F N * F P ) / ( T P + F P ) * ( F P + T N ) + ( T P + F N ) * ( F N + T N ) ) < 0 = no agreement 0–0.20 = slight 0.21–0.40 = fair 0.41–0.60 = moderate 0.61–0.80 = substantial 0.81–1 = almost perfect Segmentation Dice coefficient Measure of overlap between model and reference standard segmentations 2 T P / ( F P + 2 T P + F N ) Ranges from 0 to 1 with 1 indicating greatest similarity between model and reference segmentations Average symmetric surface distance Measure of average segmentation surface of model, S, that varies from reference standard surface segmentation, G 1 N S + N G ( ∑ x S ∈ S d ( x S , G ) + ∑ x G ∈ G d ( x G , S ) ) Smaller is better Maximum surface distance Measure of maximum segmentation surface distance between model and reference standard segmentations m a x m a x x s ε S d x S , G , m a x x G ε G d x G , S Smaller is better Volumetric overlap error Measure of degree of overlap between model and reference standard segmentation 100 ( 1 − M : R I n t e r s e c t i o n M : R U n i o n ) Smaller is better Open in a new tab TP number of true positives, TN number of true negatives, P number of positive samples, N number of negative samples, FP number of false positives, FN number of false negatives, N/A non-applicable, Ns number of predicted surface voxels, Ng number of ground truth surface voxels, d v , S = m i n s \ e p s i l o n S v − s the shortest Euclidean distance of an arbitrary voxel v to a surface S, M:R Intersection intersection between the model and reference standard segmentations, M:R Union union between the model and reference standard segmentation Deep learning knee cartilage lesion detection Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) has been shown to have high specificity but only moderate sensitivity for detecting cartilage lesions within the knee [ 7 , 8 ]. Diagnostic performance is highly dependent on the level of reader expertise, and only moderate inter-observer agreement between readers has been reported in most studies [ 7 ]. Developing standardized machine-based methods for detecting cartilage lesions on MRI would be beneficial to maximize diagnostic performance while reducing subjectivity, variability, and errors due to distraction and fatigue associated with human interpretation of imaging studies. Liu et al. used a VGG-16 CNN to segment cartilage on sagittal fat-suppressed T2-weighted two-dimensional (2D) turbo spin-echo (TSE) images and extract cartilage image patches in approximately 100 regions of interest placed on the articular surface of the femur and tibia in 175 subjects. The extracted patches were then analyzed by a VGG-16 classification CNN to determine the presence or absence of cartilage lesions using the interpretation of an experienced musculoskeletal radiologist as the reference standard. Two individual evaluations were performed using separate holdout testing datasets consisting of 1310 cartilage patches. The DL method had an AUC of 0.92 with 84% sensitivity and 85% specificity for evaluation 1 and an AUC of 0.91 with 81% sensitivity and 88% specificity for evaluation 2. There was also good intra-observer agreement between the two evaluations performed by the CNN with a kappa value of 0.76 [ 9 ]. Pedoia et al. used a U-Net CNN to segment patellar cartilage on sagittal three-dimensional (3D) fat-suppressed proton density (PD)-weighted TSE images in 1478 subjects. The segmented cartilage was then analyzed by a custom-made classification CNN to determine the presence or absence of cartilage lesions using the interpretation of an experienced musculoskeletal radiologist as the reference standard. Using a hold-out testing dataset consisting of 222 patellar cartilage surfaces, the DL method had an AUC of 0.88 with 80% sensitivity and specificity for detecting cartilage lesions [ 10 ]. After further refinement, the CNN architecture was used to detect cartilage lesions on all articular surfaces of the knee using the same image dataset. Using a hold-out testing dataset consisting of 222 knees, the DL method had an AUC of 0.93 with 85% sensitivity and 89% specificity for detecting cartilage lesions. Multi-class categorization of cartilage lesions achieved a sensitivity of 89% for intact cartilage, 72% for partial-thickness cartilage lesions, and 76% for fullthickness cartilage lesions [ 11 ]. Deep learning knee osteoarthritis diagnosis X-rays are typically used in clinical practice and research studies to establish a structural diagnosis of knee OA and to monitor disease progression. The presence and severity of radiographic knee OA are most commonly estimated using semi-quantitative scoring scales such as the Kellgren-Lawrence (KL) system [ 12 ], which is a composite measure of osteophyte formation and joint space narrowing, and the Osteoarthritis Research Society International (OARSI) atlas system [ 13 ], which assesses osteophyte formation and joint space narrowing separately. However, grading knee OA on X-rays by even experienced human readers is time-consuming and has only moderate intra-reader and inter-reader agreement [ 14 – 16 ]. Thus, there has been a recent interest in developing machine-based methods to detect and grade the severity of radiographic knee OA. These standardized machine-based approaches could improve the efficiency and reduce the variability of diagnosing knee OA on X-rays and allow human readers to concentrate their efforts on detecting other more clinically relevant findings such as fractures and neoplastic processes. Antony et al. were the first to propose a DL-based approach to detect and grade the severity of knee OA on posterior-anterior knee X-rays according to the KL system in subjects with or at risk for knee OA. A VGG-M-128 CNN was trained on 6224 knees and evaluated on a hold-out testing dataset of 2668 knees in the Osteoarthritis Initiative (OAI) database using the central reading interpretation of experienced musculoskeletal radiologists as the reference standard. The DL method achieved a multi-class accuracy of 67% for assigning the same KL grade as the reference standard, which was much higher than the multi-class accuracy of 29% for a widely used Wndchrm non-CNN-based classifier for biological images [ 17 ]. Norman et al. used a Dense-Net CNN to detect and grade the severity of knee OA on posterior-anterior knee X-rays according to the KL system in subjects with or at risk for knee OA. The CNN was trained on 33,652 knees and evaluated on a hold-out testing dataset of 5941 knees in the OAI database using the central reading interpretation of experienced musculoskeletal radiologists as the reference standard. The DL method achieved a sensitivity of 84%, 70%, 69%, and 86% and specificity of 86%, 84%, 97%, and 99% for detecting KL grade 0 or 1, KL grade 2, KL grade 3, and KL 4 knee OA, respectively. Fifty-four randomly selected knees with discordant interpretations between the CNN and reference standard were reviewed by another experienced musculoskeletal radiologist. The radiologist agreed with the KL grade assigned by the reference standard in 33 knees, the KL grade assigned by the CNN in 16 cases, and neither the KL grade assigned by the reference standard nor CNN in 16 cases [ 18 ]. Tiulpin et al. used a deep Siamese CNN architecture to detect and grade the severity of knee OA on posterior-anterior knee X-rays according to the KL system in subjects with or at risk for knee OA. The CNN was trained on 6852 knees in the OAI database and evaluated on a hold-out testing dataset of 3000 knees in the OAI database using the central reading interpretation of experienced musculoskeletal radiologists as the reference standard. The DL method achieved a weighted kappa coefficient of 0.83 and multiclass accuracy of 67% for assigning the same KL grade as the reference standard and AUC of 0.93 for detecting the presence of radiographic knee OA, which was defined as a KL grade 2 or higher [ 19 ]. Thomas et al. used a Dense-Net CNN to detect and grade the severity of knee OA on posterior-anterior knee X-rays according to the KL system in subjects with or at risk for knee OA. The CNN was trained on 36,190 knees and evaluated on a hold-out testing dataset of 4090 knees in the OAI database using the central reading interpretation of experienced musculoskeletal radiologists as the reference standard. The DL method had a precision, recall, F1-score, and accuracy of 68%, 64%, 64%, and 66%, respectively for assigning the same KL grade as the reference standard. The diagnostic performance of the CNN was further compared with two experienced musculoskeletal radiologists on a hold-out testing dataset of 50 knees in the OAI database. The weighted kappa coefficient of 0.86 between the interpretation of the CNN and each musculoskeletal radiologist for assigning the same KL grade was identical to the 0.86 weighted kappa coefficient for inter-observer agreement between the musculoskeletal radiologists [ 20 ]. Swiecicki et al. used a custom-made multi-input CNN architecture to simultaneously analyze posterior-anterior and lateral knee X-rays to detect and grade the severity of knee OA according to the KL system in subjects with or at risk for knee OA. The CNN was trained on 4598 knees in the OAI database and evaluated on a hold-out testing dataset of 1006 knees in the Multicenter Osteoarthritis Study (MOST) database using central reading interpretation of experienced musculoskeletal radiologists as the reference standard. The DL method had a weighted kappa coefficient of 0.91 and average multi-class accuracy of 72% for assigning the same KL grade as the reference standard. The diagnostic performance of the CNN was further compared with five experienced musculoskeletal radiologists on a hold-out testing dataset of 204 knees in the MOST database. The mean weighted kappa coefficient of 0.77 between the interpretation of the CNN and each musculoskeletal radiologist was higher than the 0.75 mean weighted kappa coefficient for inter-observer agreement between all pairs of musculoskeletal radiologists [ 21 ]. Kim et al. used a ResNet CNN to detect and grade the severity of knee OA on posterior-anterior knee radiographs according to the KL system in patients with knee pain being evaluated in clinical practice. The CNN was trained on 3850 knees and evaluated on a hold-out testing dataset of 516 knees using the interpretation of an experienced musculoskeletal radiologist as the reference standard. The DL method achieved an AUC for assigning the same KL grade as the reference standard of 0.91, 0.80, 0.69, 0.86, and 0.96 for KL grades 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4, knee OA respectively. A second neural network was then used to concatenate the five probability outputs for each KL grade obtained from analyzing the knee X-rays with clinical risk factors. Analyzing image data with additional patient information resulted in a significant improvement in diagnostic performance compared to analyzing image data alone [ 22 ]. Tiulpin et al. used an ensemble of two individual squeeze excitation ResNet 50 CNNs to detect and grade the severity of knee OA on posterior-anterior knee X-rays according to the KL system and OARSI atlas system in subjects with or at risk for knee OA. The CNNs were trained on 19,704 knees in the OAI database and evaluated on a hold-out testing dataset of 11,743 knees in the MOST database using the central reading interpretation of experienced musculoskeletal radiologists as the reference standard. The DL method achieved a weighted kappa coefficient of 0.82 for assigning the same KL grade as the reference standard and weighted kappa coefficients of 0.79, 0.84, 0.94, 0.83, 0.84, and 0.90 for assigning the same femoral osteophyte, tibial osteophytes, and joint space narrowing scores for the medial and lateral knee compartments, respectively. The DL method had an AUC and average precision of 0.98 for detecting the presence of radiographic knee OA, which was defined as a KL 2 grade or higher [ 23 ]. It is difficult to compare the different DL approaches for the diagnosis of knee OA on X-rays due to differences in CNNs used for image analysis, training and testing dataset sizes, and methods used to assess diagnostic performance. However, studies have shown that DL approaches have higher diagnostic performance for detecting and grading the severity of knee OA than non-CNN-based image classifiers [ 17 ] and that the agreement between the interpretation of the CNNs and experienced musculoskeletal radiologists is similar to the inter-observer agreement between the musculoskeletal radiologists for assigning a KL grade [ 20 , 21 ]. Deep learning knee cartilage segmentation Tissue segmentation of knee MRI datasets is a fundamental step in the image analysis performed in many OA research studies. Traditionally, this is done by having users scroll through stacks of images and manually delineate the boundaries of knee structures on each image slice. However, manual tissue segmentation is time-consuming, and its efficiency and repeatability are influenced by the level of human expertise [ 24 ]. Fully automated techniques based on active shape modeling [ 25 ] and atlas databases [ 26 ] have been described for segmenting cartilage and bone on knee MRI datasets and have shown promising results. However, these methods rely on a priori knowledge of knee shapes from many subjects and require high computation costs due to the need to access databases and continuously compare current detected knee shapes to pre-stored shapes. Prasoon et al. were the first to propose a DL-based segmentation approach for knee MRI datasets using a CNN-based voxel classification method. An image voxel was identified as either cartilage or background by combining the results of three simple CNNs, each of which was applied in a separate coronal, sagittal, and axial plane. The tri-planar CNNs were trained and evaluated on 139 knee MRI datasets and achieved promising initial results with a Dice coefficient of 0.82 for segmenting tibia cartilage compared to the manually segmented reference standard [ 27 ]. Liu et al. implemented a SegNet CNN for segmenting cartilage and bone on multi-contrast knee MRI datasets. The CNN was trained and evaluated on three knee datasets including sagittal fat-suppressed 3D T1-weighted spoiled gradient recalled-echo (3D-SPGR) images in 100 subjects in the Segmentation of Knee Images 2010 dataset, sagittal fat-suppressed PD-weighted 3D-TSE images in 60 clinical patients, and sagittal T2 mapping images in 100 clinical patients. The DL method had average symmetric surface distances of 0.56 mm and 0.50 mm for femur and tibia bone, respectively, and volumetric overlap errors of 28.4% and 33.1% for femur and tibia cartilage, respectively, for the 3D-SPGR images compared to manually segmented reference standard. The DL approach showed similar high segmentation accuracy for the 3D-TSE and T2 mapping image datasets [ 28 ]. The same CNN architecture was combined with a spatial image post-processing algorithm to segment 12 different knee tissues on sagittal fat-suppressed PD-weighted 3D-TSE knee MRI datasets in 20 clinical patients. The DL method had mean Dice coefficients above 0.90 for femur, tibia, and muscle and mean Dice coefficients between 0.80 and 0.90 for femur cartilage, tibia cartilage, patella, patellar cartilage, meniscus, quadriceps and patellar tendons, and infrapatellar fat pad. Only joint effusion and popliteal cyst had mean Dice coefficients below 0.80 compared to the manually segmented reference standard [ 29 ]. Ambellan et al. described a more complex DL architecture for segmenting cartilage and bone on multi-contrast knee MRI datasets. The DL approach used a 2D U-Net CNN to create initial segmentation masks for the femur and tibia bone followed by a 3D U-Net CNN to segment small sub-volumes at the bone surface to provide better classification accuracy. The outputs of both CNNs were further refined by statistical shape modeling to improve classification accuracy. The CNN architecture was trained and evaluated on sagittal fat-suppressed 3D-SPGR images in 100 subjects in the Segmentation of Knee Images 2010 dataset and on sagittal fat-suppressed 3D dual-echo in the steady-state (3D-DESS) images in 87 subjects in the OAI database. The DL method had average symmetric surface distances of 0.43 mm and 0.35 mm for femur and tibia bone, respectively, and volumetric overlap errors of 20% and 19% for femur and tibia cartilage, respectively, compared to the manually segmented reference standard for the 3D-SPGR images and average symmetric surface distances of 0.17 and 0.18 for femur and tibia bone, respectively, and volumetric overlap errors of 18% and 25% for femur and tibia cartilage, respectively, for the 3D-DESS images [ 30 ]. Norman et al. used a U-Net CNN for segmenting cartilage, bone, and menisci on multi-contrast knee MRI datasets. The CNN was trained and evaluated on two knee datasets, including sagittal fat-suppressed 3D-SPGR images in 464 subjects enrolled in internal research studies and sagittal fat-suppressed 3D-DESS images in 87 subjects in the OAI database. The DL method had mean Dice coefficients of 0.74 and 0.77 for cartilage and meniscus, respectively, on the 3D-SPGR images and mean Dice coefficients of 0.74 to 0.77 for cartilage and meniscus, respectively, on the 3D-DESS images compared to manually segmented reference standard. Furthermore, the mean correlation coefficients between manual and DL-based quantification of cartilage thickness and cartilage volume were 0.93 and 0.94, respectively [ 31 ]. Gaj et al. used a conditional generative adversarial network to improve the accuracy of a U-Net CNN for segmenting cartilage and meniscus on knee MRI datasets. The CNN was trained and evaluated on sagittal fat-suppressed 3D-DESS images in 176 subjects in the OAI database. The DL method achieved a mean Dice coefficient of 0.88 for both cartilage and meniscus compared to the manually segmented reference standard [ 32 ]. The segmentation accuracy of the same CNN architecture was further evaluated on knee MRI datasets consisting of sagittal fat-suppressed PD-weighted 2D-TSE images performed on 30 clinical patients using different MRI scanners and imaging parameters. A transfer learning model pre-trained on the MRI datasets in the OAI database achieved a mean Dice coefficient of 0.82 and a mean symmetric surface distance of 0.35 mm for segmenting cartilage [ 33 ]. Kessler et al. compared a U-Net CNN with a conditional generative adversarial network with a U-Net generator and patch-based discriminator for segmenting cartilage, bone, and meniscus on multi-contrast knee MRI datasets. The CNN was trained and evaluated on three knee datasets including sagittal fat-suppressed 3D-SPGR images in 100 subjects in the Segmentation of Knee Images 2010 dataset, sagittal fat-suppressed 3D-DESS images in 507 subjects in the OAI database, and sagittal fat-suppressed 3D-SPGR images in 10 subjects in the Advanced MRI of Osteoarthritis (AMROA) database. Evaluation of segmentation accuracy in the small AMROA database with limited training data using models pre-trained with images in the Segmentation of Knee Images dataset and OAI database showed Dice coefficients greater than 0.95 for all bone structures and greater than 0.83 for all cartilage and meniscus structures compared to the manually segmented reference standard. The simple U-Net CNN showed similar segmentation accuracy as the optimized conditional generative adversarial network for all knee structures [ 34 ]. Panfilov et al. used a CNN architecture based on U-Net with a pre-trained VGG19 encoder for segmenting cartilage and meniscus on knee MRI datasets. The CNN was trained and evaluated on sagittal fat-suppressed 3D-DESS images in 176 subjects in the OAI database. The DL method had Dice coefficients of 0.91, 0.90, 0.87, and 0.87 for femur cartilage, tibia cartilage, patella cartilage, and meniscus, respectively, compared to the manually segmented reference standard. Furthermore, the CNN showed correlation coefficients between 0.77 and 0.96 and mean differences between 0.51 and 1.14 mm for subregional cartilage thickness measurements compared to the manually segmented reference standard [ 35 ]. Wirth et al. also used a U-Net CNN with a pre-trained VGG19 encoder for segmenting cartilage and bone on multi-contrast knee MRI datasets. The CNN was trained and evaluated on coronal fat-suppressed 3D-SPGR images and sagittal fat-suppressed 3D-DESS images in 92 subjects in the OAI database. For both the 3D-SPGR and 3D-DESS images, the DL method had Dice coefficients between 0.89 and 0.92 for femur and tibia cartilage and femur and tibia bone and similar scan-scan repeatability compared to the manually segmented reference standard [ 36 ]. The segmentation accuracy of the same CNN architecture was further evaluated on sagittal fat-suppressed 3D-DESS images from 600 subjects with or at risk for knee OA in the National Institute of Health OA Biomarker Consortium. The DL segmentation approach showed similar sensitivity to change as the manually segmented reference standard for longitudinal cartilage thickness loss in knee OA and a similar ability to differentiate longitudinal rates of cartilage thickness loss between subjects with different progression profiles [ 37 ]. Multiple DL approaches have been shown to provide accurate segmentation of cartilage on each image slice using a wide variety of knee MRI datasets as summarized in Table 3 . Almost all DL methods have used 2D CNN architectures for cartilage segmentation. While 3D CNNs may theoretically provide more accurate cartilage segmentation by analyzing through-plane image information, they are difficult to implement due to the higher computational load and more image data required for model training. It is difficult to compare the different DL approaches for fully automated segmentation of knee MRI datasets due to differences in sequences and parameters used for image acquisition, CNNs used for image analysis, training and testing dataset sizes, and methods used to assess segmentation accuracy. However, for the studies that used the sagittal fat-suppressed 3D-SPGR images in the Segmentation of Knee Images 2010 dataset, all DL approaches achieved higher accuracy for segmenting knee cartilage and bone than conventional model-based and atlas-based methods with substantial reductions in segmentation times [ 28 , 34 , 38 ]. Desai et al. recently performed a multi-institutional knee MRI segmentation challenge using sagittal fat-suppressed 3D-DESS images in 88 subjects in the OAI database for training and evaluation. The results showed no difference for the four top performing CNNs in any performance metric for segmenting cartilage and meniscus compared to manually segmented reference standard, indicating that diverse CNN architectures have similar ability to segment knee MRI datasets [ 39 ]. Table 3. MRI sequences and convolutional neural network architectures used in deep learning models for classification and segmentation applications in osteoarthritis imaging Application Sequence CNN Performance Knee cartilage lesion detection Liu et al. [ 9 ] Fat-Sat T2 2D-TSE VGG-16 AUC = 0.91 Pedoia et al. [ 11 ] Fat-Sat PD 3D-TSE U-Net AUC = 0.93 Knee cartilage segmentation Liu et al. [ 28 ] Fat-Sat 3D-SPGR SegNet ASSD = 0.50–0.65 mm VOE = 28–33% Zhou et al. [ 29 ] Fat-Sat PD 3D-TSE SegNet Dice = 0.80–0.90 Ambellan et al. [ 30 ] Fat-Sat 3D-SPGR 2D U-Net and 3D U-Net ASSD = 0.35–0.43 mm Fat-Sat 3D-DESS VOE = 19.0–20% ASSD = 0.17–0.18 VOE = 18.0–25.0% Normal et al. [ 31 ] Fat-Sat 3D-SPGR U-Net Dice = 0.74 Fat-Sat 3D-DESS Dice = 0.74 Gaj et al. [ 32 ] Fat-Sat 3D-DESS UNet and GAN Dice = 0.88 Fat-Sat PD 2D-TSE Dice = 0.82 ASSD = 0.35 mm Kessler et al. [ 34 ] Fat-Sat 3D-SPGR U-Net Dice ≥ 0.83 Panfilov et al. [ 35 ] Fat-Sat 3D-DESS U-Net Dice = 0.87–0.91 Wirth et al. [ 36 ] Fat-Sat 3D-SPGR U-Net Dice = 0.89–0.92 Fat-Sat 3D-DESS Dice = 0.89–0.92 Prediction of progression of knee joint space loss Schiratti et al PD 2D-TSE EfficientNet AUC = 0.59 Prediction of progression to total knee replacement Tolpadi et al. [ 83 ] Fat-Sat 3D-DESS DenseNet AUC = 0.89 Wang et al. [ 84 ] Fat-Sat PD 2D-TSE Custom 3D CNN AUC = 0.86 Fat-Sat 3D-DESS AUC = 0.86 Prediction of progression of knee pain Chang et al. [ 86 ] Fat-Sat PD 2D-TSE Siamese CNN AUC = 0.77 Lee et al. [ 87 ] Fat-Sat PD 2D-TSE DenseNet AUC = 0.80 Open in a new tab Fat-Sat fat-suppressed, T2 T2 -weighted, PD proton density-weighted, 2D-TSE two-dimensional turbo spinecho, 3D-TSE three-dimensional turbo spin-echo, 3D-SPGR three-dimensional spoiled gradient recalledecho, 3D-DESS three-dimensional dual-echo in the steady-state, 2D two-dimensional, 3D three-dimensional, CNN convolutional neural network, AUC area under the receiver operating characteristic curve, ASSD average symmetric surface distance, VOE volumetric overlap error, Dice dice coefficient Deep learning knee osteoarthritis risk assessment There is an important need to identify individuals at high risk for knee OA incidence and progression. Conservative treatment options including weight loss [ 40 – 42 ], aerobic activity [ 43 – 47 ], and range of motion and strengthening exercises [ 44 , 45 , 47 – 50 ] may alleviate symptoms in patients with knee OA but are most effective when initiated during the early stages of the disease [ 51 ]. Thus, a preventive strategy that identifies individuals at high risk for knee OA progression prior to the onset of chronic knee pain and advanced joint degeneration would enhance the effectiveness of current conservative interventions. Furthermore, X-rays and MRI currently used to assess knee OA progression in clinical drug trials have relative responsiveness to change over time [ 52 – 55 ], resulting in the need for large numbers of subjects and long follow-up periods to investigate the efficacy of new disease-modifying OA therapies. Identifying individuals with knee OA at high risk for progression would help select the best subjects for inclusion in clinical trials, which would decrease costs and increase the likelihood of effective drug development [ 56 – 61 ]. The Foundation of the National Institute of Health Biomarkers Consortium Osteoarthritis Project has recently been established to identify the most responsive imaging biomarkers for knee OA using data from the OAI [ 62 ]. Quantitative and semi-quantitative parameters on baseline X-rays, including KL grade [ 63 , 64 ], joint space width [ 65 ], and joint alignment [ 66 ], and quantitative and semi-quantitative parameters on MRI, including cartilage lesions [ 67 , 68 ], effusion [ 67 – 71 ], meniscus tears [ 67 , 68 , 72 , 73 ], bone marrow edema lesions [ 74 – 77 ], and Hoffa’s fat pad synovitis [ 67 , 71 , 78 ], have been shown to be associated with knee OA incidence and progression. However, evaluating these structural features on imaging studies is time-consuming and reader-dependent, making them difficult to incorporate into clinically useful OA risk assessment models. DL has tremendous potential for creating comprehensive OA risk assessment models by providing a new rapid and fully automated method to extract useful prognostic information from imaging studies. Tiulpin et al. used a custom-made CNN architecture consisting of a convolutional block and two fully connected blocks to analyze baseline posterior-anterior knee X-rays to predict radiographic OA progression in subjects with or at risk for knee OA. Knee OA progression was defined as an increase in KL grade over an 84-month follow-up period. The CNN was trained on 4928 knees in the OAI database and evaluated on a hold-out testing dataset of 3918 knees in the MOST database. The DL model had an AUC of 0.79 and average precision of 0.68 for predicting knee OA progression. The diagnostic performance of the DL model was significantly higher than a traditional model analyzing baseline clinical and radiographic risk factors, which achieved an AUC of 0.75 and average precision of 0.62 [ 79 ]. Guan et al. used a DenseNet CNN to analyze baseline posterior-anterior knee X-rays to predict radiographic OA progression in subjects with or at risk for knee OA. Knee OA progression was defined as a greater than 0.7-mm decrease in minimum medial joint space width measurement over a 60-month follow-up period. The CNN was trained on 1400 knees and evaluated on a hold-out testing dataset of 400 knees in the OAI database. The DL model had an AUC of 0.80 with 78% sensitivity and 76% specificity for predicting knee OA progression. The diagnostic performance of the DL model was significantly higher than a traditional model analyzing baseline clinical and radiographic risk factors, which had an AUC of 0.66 with 62% sensitivity and 64% specificity. A combined joint training model was also developed that extracted DL information from baseline X-rays as a feature vector, which was further concatenated with the information extracted from clinical and radiographic risk factor data. The combined joint training model had an AUC of 0.86 with 81% sensitivity and specificity, which was significantly higher than the AUCs of both the traditional and DL models [ 80 ]. Schiratti et al. used an EfficientNet CNN to analyze coronal PD-weighted 2D-TSE images on baseline MRI along with clinical and radiographic risk factors to predict radiographic OA progression in subjects with or at risk for knee OA. Knee OA progression was defined as a greater than 0.5-mm decrease in minimum medial joint space width measurement over a 12-month follow-up period. The CNN was trained and evaluated on 9280 knee MRI datasets with corresponding clinical and radiographic risk factors in the OAI database using fivefold cross-validation with the imaging studies of subjects at multiple time points included to increase the sample size. The DL model had an AUC of 0.59, average precision of 0.13, and an average recall of 0.84 for predicting knee OA progression [ 81 ]. Leung et al. used a ResNet CNN to analyze baseline posterior-anterior knee X-rays to predict total knee replacement in subjects with or at risk for knee OA over a 108-month follow-up. The CNN was trained and evaluated using sevenfold nested cross-validation on a balanced case control cohort consisting of 324 knees that received total knee replacement and 324 age, sex, ethnicity, and body mass index-matched control knees that did not receive total knee replacement in the OAI database. The DL model had an AUC of 0.87 with 77% sensitivity and 83% specificity for predicting total knee replacement. The diagnostic performance of the DL model was significantly higher than a traditional model analyzing baseline KL grade, which had an AUC of 0.66 with 58% sensitivity and 91% specificity [ 82 ]. Tolpadi et al. used a DenseNet CNN to analyze either baseline posterior-anterior knee X-rays or sagittal fat-suppressed 3D-DESS images on baseline MRI to predict total knee replacement in subjects with or at risk for knee OA over a 60-month follow-up. The CNN was trained on 31,046 knees and evaluated on a hold-out testing dataset of 5478 knees in the OAI database with the imaging studies of subjects at multiple time points included to increase the sample size. The DL model analyzing baseline X-rays had an AUC of 0.85 with 73% sensitivity and 87% specificity for predicting total knee replacement, while the DL model analyzing baseline MRI had an AUC of 0.89 with 85% sensitivity and 82% specificity. In comparison, a traditional model analyzing baseline clinical and radiographic risk factors had an AUC of 0.87. Linear regression models that included the prediction output of the DL models analyzing either baseline X-rays or MRI along with clinical and radiographic risk factors had an AUC of 0.89 for X-rays and 0.83 for MRI [ 83 ]. Wang et al. used a custom-made 3D CNN architecture consisting of multiple residual blocks to analyze either sagittal fat-suppressed 3D-DESS or sagittal fat-suppressed PD-weighted 2D-TSE images on baseline MRI to predict total knee replacement in subjects with or at risk for knee OA over a 108-month follow-up period. The CNN was trained and evaluated using sevenfold cross-validation on a balanced case–control cohort consisting of 324 knees that received total knee replacement and 324 age, sex, ethnicity, and body mass index-matched control knees that did not receive total knee replacement in the OAI database. The DL models analyzing the MRI datasets with different tissue contrast both had an AUC of 0.86. The diagnostic performance of the DL model was significantly higher than a traditional model analyzing clinical and radiographic risk factors, which had an AUC of 0.77. A linear regression model that included the prediction outputs of both DL models analyzing baseline MRI along with clinical and radiographic risk factors had an AUC of 0.88, which was significantly higher than the AUCs of both the traditional and DL models [ 84 ]. Guan et al. used a DenseNet CNN to analyze baseline posterior-anterior knee X-rays to predict knee pain progression in subjects with or at risk for knee OA. Pain progression was defined as a 9-point or greater increase in Western Ontario and McMaster Universities Osteoarthritis Index (WOMAC) pain scores between baseline and two or more follow-up time points over 48 months The CNN was trained on 4200 knees and evaluated on a hold-out testing dataset of 500 knees in the OAI database. The DL model had an AUC of 0.77 with 77% sensitivity and 71% specificity for predicting pain progression. The diagnostic performance of the DL model was significantly higher than a traditional model analyzing baseline clinical and radiographic risk factors, which achieved an AUC of 0.69 with 67% sensitivity and 64% specificity. A combined joint training model that included DL analysis of baseline radiographs along with clinical and radiographic risk factors had an AUC of 0.81 with 72% sensitivity and 81% specificity, which was significantly higher than the AUCs of both the traditional and DL models [ 85 ]. Chang et al. used a Siamese neural network to analyze sagittal fat-suppressed PD-weighted 2D-TSE images on baseline MRI of both knees of subjects with or at risk for knee OA who had unilateral knee pain. The Siamese network architecture simultaneously analyzed similarly located image slices of both knees of each subject during model training to distinguish between the symptomatic and asymptomatic knee. The CNN was trained and evaluated on 1505 subjects in the OAI database using tenfold cross-validation. The DL model had an AUC of 0.81 for distinguishing the symptomatic knee from the asymptomatic knee [ 86 ]. Lee et al. used a DenseNet CNN to analyze sagittal fat-suppressed PD-weighted 2D-TSE images on baseline MRI along with clinical risk factors to predict the presence of knee pain and the temporal pattern of progression of knee pain in subjects with or at risk for knee OA. Functional principal component analysis identified two distinct subtypes of temporal progression of knee pain over time, which included a stable pain trajectory in 87% of subjects and a worsening pain projector in 13% of subjects. The CNN was trained on 11,684 knees and evaluated on a hold-out testing dataset of 2921 knees in the OAI database with the imaging studies of subjects at multiple time points included to increase the sample size. The DL model had an AUC of 0.90 with 81% sensitivity and 82% specificity for predicting the presence of knee pain. The DL model had an AUC of 0.80 with 50% sensitivity and 85% specificity for distinguishing between knees with a stable or worsening temporal progression of knee pain [ 87 ]. Pedoia et al. used a DenseNet CNN to analyze sagittal T2 mapping images on baseline MRI to detect the presence of baseline radiographic knee OA. Voxel-based relaxometry was first used for quantification and voxel-based analysis of the differences in cartilage T2 relaxation times between subjects with and without radiographic knee OA. The CNN was then used to detect knee OA from the T2 relaxation time data along with clinical risk factors. The CNN was trained on 4077 knees and evaluated on a hold-out testing dataset of 720 knees in the OAI database. The DL model trained on voxel-based relaxometry of cartilage T2 relaxation time along with clinical risk factors had an AUC of 0.77 with 67% sensitivity and 82% specificity for predicting the presence of radiographic knee OA. In comparison, a DL model trained on raw cartilage T2 relaxation time data had a significantly higher diagnostic performance with an AUC of 0.83 with 77% sensitivity and 78% specificity [ 88 ]. Multiple DL approaches have been shown to provide accurate prediction of knee OA progression using knee radiographs and different knee MRI datasets as summarized in Table 3 . It is difficult to compare the different DL approaches for creating knee OA risk assessment models due to differences in imaging modality analyzed, techniques used for image acquisition, CNNs used for image analysis, training and testing dataset sizes, outcome measure used to define knee OA progression, and methods used to assess model performance. However, a review of the literature as a whole suggests several important findings. First, DL models analyzing baseline imaging studies showed significantly higher diagnostic performance than traditional models analyzing baseline clinical and radiographic risk factors for predicting knee OA progression [ 79 , 80 , 82 – 85 ]. In addition, regardless of the imaging modality analyzed, DL models for predicting total knee replacement [ 82 – 84 ] had higher diagnostic performance than DL models for predicting radiographic progression [ 79 – 81 ], and both DL models for predicting total knee replacement and radiographic progression had higher diagnostic performance than DL models for predicting pain progression [ 85 , 87 ]. Finally, models combining DL analysis of baseline imaging studies with baseline clinical and radiographic risk factors showed significant improvement in diagnostic performance compared to DL models analyzing baseline imaging studies alone and traditional models analyzing baseline clinical and radiographic risk factors alone [ 80 , 84 , 85 ]. Future directions The preliminary results on DL applications in knee cartilage lesion detection on MRI and knee OA diagnosis on X-rays have been encouraging. However, most studies have been performed using a single subject population that was evaluated using the same X-ray and MRI systems with the same imaging parameters. The translatability of DL models created in these homogenous environments into clinical practice and research studies that use a wider variety of imaging equipment and techniques needs to be further investigated. This is especially true for MRI, where image quality varies greatly across different scanner types and different imaging protocols. However, even for X-rays, the positioning devices utilized in the OAI and MOST to standardize knee positioning are not typically used in clinical practice, and thus, the influence of this factor on model performance needs to be further investigated. In addition, the usefulness of DL detection of knee cartilage lesions on MRI and DL grading of composite and individual features of knee joint degeneration on X-rays requires additional investigation. Since radiologists still need to interpret these imaging studies as part of routine clinical care and for safety monitoring in research studies, the ability of DL models to improve diagnostic performance and enhance work efficiency needs to be further evaluated. The preliminary results on DL applications for knee cartilage segmentation on MRI have been extremely encouraging. DL approaches have been shown to achieve higher accuracy for fully automated segmentation of knee cartilage and bone than currently used model-based and atlas-based methods with substantial reductions in segmentation times [ 28 , 34 , 38 ]. However, the generalizability of DL segmentation models needs to be further investigated. Transfer learning has been shown to be effective for optimizing DL methods for segmenting knee MRI datasets with different tissue contrasts [ 28 , 30 , 31 , 34 , 36 ]. However, additional model training on the new image datasets is still required, which would be difficult to perform in research studies and for each individual scanner at each individual institution in clinical practice. Future studies are needed to investigate the accuracy of DL approaches for segmenting more heterogeneous knee MRI datasets and to develop improved CNN architectures and training methods to reduce model sensitivity to potential fluctuations in image quality. Knee OA risk assessment is one of the most exciting DL applications in OA imaging as it accomplishes an important clinical task that cannot be performed by human beings. The initial results have been encouraging. However, limitations of current OA risk assessment models using DL analysis of baseline X-rays and MRI include less than optimal diagnostic performance with areas under the curve under 0.9 and unequal sensitivities and specificities that typically vary with the severity of knee joint degeneration. Thus, there is an important need to maximize the diagnostic performance of OA risk assessment models by combining the most relevant clinical and radiographic risk factors with simultaneous DL analysis of multiple image datasets. Most previous studies have used ensembling methods or simple machine learning approaches to combine the outcome predictions of individual models analyzing clinical and radiographic risk factors and different image datasets. However, future work is needed to investigate alternative approaches for simultaneous analysis of clinical and radiographic risk factors, images from different time points, images with different tissue contrasts, and images from different yet complementary modalities in the same CNN architecture. For example, knowledge distillation and attention-based fusion approaches have been shown to be superior to ensemble methods in DL prediction models combining information from multiple image datasets [ 89 , 90 ]. Investigation of the translatability of OA risk assessment models to the more heterogenous MRI datasets typically acquired in clinical practice and research studies is also needed. In addition, all previous DL risk assessment models have been trained and evaluated using the OAI and MOST databases, which are primarily composed of older, overweight Caucasian subjects. Thus, model generalizability to more age, body mass index, race, and ethnically diverse populations needs further investigation. Key points. Deep learning techniques have similar diagnostic performance as human readers for detecting and grading cartilage lesions within the knee on MRI. Deep learning methods can detect and grade the severity of knee osteoarthritis and features of knee osteoarthritis on X-rays with similar diagnostic performance as human readers. Deep learning approaches for full-automated segmentation of cartilage and other knee tissue can achieve higher segmentation accuracy than currently used methods with substantial reductions in segmentation times. Deep learning models have high diagnostic performance for predicting osteoarthritis outcomes, including the incidence and progression of radiographic knee osteoarthritis, the presence and progression of knee pain, and future total knee replacement. Footnotes Declarations Conflict of interest The authors declare no competing interests. References 1. Erickson BJ, Korfiatis P, Akkus Z, Kline TL. Machine learning for medical imaging. Radiographics. 2017;37(2):505–15. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Binvignat M, Pedoia V, Butte AJ, Louati K, Klatzmann D, Berenbaum F, et al. Use of machine learning in osteoarthritis research: a systematic literature review. Rmd Open. 2022;8(1):e001998. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. LeCun Y, Bengio Y, Hinton G. Deep learning. Nature. 2015;521(7553):436–44. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Bedson J, Jordan K, Croft P. The prevalence and history of knee osteoarthritis in general practice: a case-control study. Fam Pract. 2005;22(1):103–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google