INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5045,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11293168','High-resolution oblique coronal MRI at optimal flexed-knee angle: a novel imaging method for enhanced anterior cruciate ligament tear diagnosis.','Chen C, Liu X, Wu P, Liang Q, Peng S, Hu P, Liao Y.','2024','flexed flexed flexed laxity intercondylar attaches hyperextension gokalp sharper finer flexed xiangya s289 ingenia homemade zl201610430464 flexed straps homemade flexed spair mm² nsa straps spair tense condyles spair spair spair spair mm² nsa pdwi spair mm² nsa nondiagnostic reevaluated mariakerke mariakerke flexed basketball twists basketball sprain badminton tennis ocor ocor twisting tensioned twisted twisted ocor tearing flexed tearing ocor ocor ocor ocor twisting 30º nenezic kocijancic przeworski intercondylar gradations flexed flexed spair nsa pdwi pzh yjl yjl yjl yjl s2020jjmsxm2267 xiangya s289 zantop blo 0b013e31802b4a59 blo 0b013e31802b4a59 smigielski zdanowicz drwiega ciszek 620x 98b8 620x 98b8 kaeding magnussen csm csm okuwaki filardo andriolo frattura zaffagnini candrian bruise arthrosc s00167 s00167 bordoni frattura previtali tamborini candrian cristallo lacalamita bruise taljanovic gmitro krupinski s12891 s12891 hoogeslag buitenhuis derks rph raak veld s0030 s0030 gokalp demirag aydemir yazici anteromedial posterolateral nakase toratani ohashi kitaoka paunipagar wjo wjo cotten delfaut demondion lapegue boukhelifa boutry muhle dieke springerplus patellar stenotic intercondylar s13018 s13018 dome awh rennirt intercondylar ramnath mric mric boxheimer treiber goepfert marincek menisci blaise remen ambarki weiland kuehn orry mintz difelice halinen koivikko lindahl hirvensalo s00264 s00264 difelice villegas arthro arthro intercondylar walley bridgman maffulli ldm022 ldm022 lars etm etm w199 nenezic kocijancic raon raon przeworski adamiak nowicki mieszkowska tobolska głodek jvetres jvetres mccall maffulli bridgman mltj mltj tipoe smigielski zdanowicz drwiega ciszek 620x 98b8 620x 98b8','Abstract Background The accuracy of traditional knee MR imaging in diagnosing anterior cruciate ligament tears, especially partial tears, is relatively low, which may lead to misdiagnosis and missed diagnosis. This study aimed to assess the diagnostic performance of a novel imaging method, high-resolution oblique coronal MRI at an optimal flexed-knee Angle, for ACL tears. Methods 50 healthy volunteers were scanned with a scan-assisted device for the optimal flexion angle of ACL. For 92 knee trauma patients selected strictly according to inclusion and exclusion criteria, conventional extended-knee scans (control group) and high-resolution oblique coronal scans based on the optimal flexed-knee angle (experimental group) were conducted. Two observers rated ACL visibility blindly on a 5-point scale. Arthroscopy-defined outcomes determined diagnostic metrics for each method and sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value, negative predictive value and accuracy were calculated. Results The average optimal flexion angle for healthy volunteers was approximately 30° (30.3° ± 5.0°). Imaging demonstrated complete visualization of the ACL in 96.7% of images in the experimental group versus 12.0% in the control group. The diagnostic indicators of the experimental group surpassed those of the control group: sensitivity (94.9% vs. 76.3%), specificity (97.0% vs. 81.8%), positive predictive value (98.2% vs. 88.2%), negative predictive value(91.4% vs. 65.9%), and accuracy (95.7% vs. 78.3%). ROC analysis indicated superior diagnostic performance in the experimental group, with an AUC of 0.945 compared with 0.776 for the control group ( p < 0.0001). Conclusions High-resolution oblique coronal imaging at the optimal 30° flexed-knee angle improved ACL visualization and diagnostic performance compared with conventional techniques. Keywords: Anterior cruciate ligament tear, Coronal positioning, Diagnostic performance, Flexion angle, High-resolution magnetic resonance imaging Introduction The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) is a key ligament that maintains the anterior laxity of the knee joint. It originates from the anterior part of the intercondylar eminence of the tibia and attaches to the posterior part of the medial surface of the lateral femoral condyle, thus preventing hyperextension of the knee joint [ 1 , 2 ]. ACL injuries are among the most common knee injuries, particularly in athletes. In the United States, more than 200,000 people suffer ACL injuries each year, accounting for more than half of all knee injuries, most of which require ligament reconstruction surgery [ 3 ]. ACL rupture can affect the stability of the knee joint, leading to joint dysfunction. Bone marrow congestion surrounding the lesion is commonly associated with ACL injury [ 4 – 6 ]; in such a case, persistent bone marrow edema can contribute to cartilage degradation, leading to severe joint injury [ 7 ]. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a convenient and noninvasive method for examining ACL tears; however, misdiagnosis or missed diagnosis in routine knee joint scans is possible [ 8 , 9 ]; accuracy can be as low as 57.1% for diagnosing partial ACL tears [ 10 ]. MRI oblique coronal scans can improve the visualization of the entire ACL and enhance the diagnostic performance for ACL tears. For example, Gokalp et al. [ 11 ] added thin-section oblique coronal images to routine knee joint sequences to improve visualization of ACL tears and determine tear levels. Similarly, Kosaka et al. [ 12 ] added oblique MRI for improving the accuracy of diagnosis of ACL tears and demonstrated reasonable efficacy in detecting residual ACL tissue. Visualization of the entire ACL also is difficult in a few patients due to the unique anatomical structure of the ACL and partial volume effects from the surrounding structures. The effect can mask or obscure the ACL and make the diagnosis of ACL tears, especially partial tears, challenging [ 13 ]. The use of various scan planes can improve visualization of the entire ACL, and coronal and axial planes have advantages over sagittal planes, especially in capturing the full morphology of the ACL [ 14 ]. However, the extended-knee position has been used in most studies, and that position may not be optimal for displaying the ACL in knee joint scans [ 9 , 15 ]. Identifying the optimal flexion angle is crucial for visualizing the ACL without distortion or overlapping from other structures, which could interfere with accurate diagnosis and treatment planning. On the other hand, the average width of the ACL in adults generally does not exceed 6 mm [ 16 , 17 ]; therefore, capturing the ACL in just 1–2 sagittal slices may miss parts of the ligament or associated pathology [ 18 ]. High-resolution coronal imaging provides detailed cross-sectional views of the knee joint, allowing for improved visualization of the intricate structures, such as the ACL. Compared with traditional imaging, this method offers sharper images with finer details, which can help in making accurate diagnoses [ 19 ]. The purpose of this study was to determine the optimal flexed-knee angle for ACL visualization and utilize high-resolution oblique coronal MR imaging based on this angle to enhance the diagnostic performance for ACL tears. Materials and methods Study participants The prospective study adhered to the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki and was approved by the Institutional Review Board of The Third Xiangya Hospital, Central South University (No:2019-S289).All participants provided signed informed consent forms. From January 2021 to June 2021, we recruited 50 healthy volunteers (25 women and 25 men) aged 15–69 years, with a mean age of (35.6 ± 13.3) years. Standard knee joint appearance was normal in all participants, who had no known history of knee-related diseases or operations. The patient group comprised individuals who underwent MRI evaluations of their knee joints from September 2021 to December 2022 for trauma-related injury. The inclusion criteria were (1) age 12–80 years; (2) history of new acute knee joint trauma and willingness for further treatment; (3) requirement for a preoperative MRI assessment; and (4) arthroscopic confirmation of an ACL tear. The exclusion criteria were (1) treatment for knee trauma before their MRI assessment and (2) low-quality MRI scans. The study selection process is depicted in Fig. 1 . Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Flow chart of patient selection Optimal flexion angle measurement All volunteers were scanned with a 3.0T MRI scanner (Ingenia, Philips Healthcare, Best, The Netherlands) fitted with an original 4-channel FLEX M coil. A homemade unique knee joint dynamic scanning device (Chinese National Invention Patent, No. ZL201610430464.5) was used to ascertain knee optimal flexion angle. The volunteers were placed in the lateral position for scanning. Their femur on the examined side was fixed, with the knee joint serving as the pivot point, and the flexed-knee angles were adjusted with the straps on the traction device, as illustrated in Fig. 2 a. There is a scale on the lower plate of the homemade device, and the flexed-knee angle is defined as the angle between the extension line of the femur’s midline and the tibia’s midline. A sagittal T2 Weighted Imaging-Turbo Spin Echo-Spectral Attenuated Inversion Recovery (T2WI-TSE-SPAIR) fast dynamic sequence was used with the parameters echo time (TE) = 60 ms, repetition time (TR) = 3000 ms, field of view (FOV) = 150 × 150 mm², matrix = 260 × 214, slice thickness = 3.5 mm, slice gap = 0.35 mm and 6 slices, Sensitivity Encoding (SENSE) factor = 1, number of signal averaged (NSA) = 1, and the scanning duration was 30 s. Two experienced physicians reviewed the scans, identified the optimal visibility layer of ACL (ACL runs nearly straight with moderate tension), and noted the flexion angle (Fig. 2 b to f). These data were statistically analyzed to determine the ideal flexion angle for the 50 volunteers. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Knee joint dynamic scanning positioning and measurement of the optimal flexion angle. a Volunteer in the lateral position, with knee joint angle adjusted using the traction straps on the self-made knee joint dynamic imaging assistive device (red arrow); b-e Sagittal scans using the T2WI-TSE-SPAIR fast dynamic sequence; f Measurement of the flexion angle at the level where the ACL runs nearly straight and is moderately tense (i.e., the individualized optimal flexion angle) Patient grouping and scanning Patients were scanned on the same MRI scanner as for the volunteers but with an 8-channel knee joint coil. Initially, conventional knee scans were performed in an extended position (control group). High-resolution coronal scans then were performed based on the previously determined optimal flexion angle (experimental group). Patients served as their own controls. Figure 3 illustrates the positioning for oblique coronal scanning. Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Scanning positioning of the oblique coronal plane. a Sagittal scanning orientation parallel to the course of the ACL; b Coronal plane aligned at approximately 30° to the joint surface, with a higher inner aspect and a lower outer aspect; c Transverse plane set at an angle of approximately 30° to the line connecting the inner and outer edges of the femoral condyles, also with a higher inner aspect and a lower outer aspect The scanning sequences for the control group included sagittal T1WI-TSE, sagittal T2WI-TSE-SPAIR, coronal T2WI-TSE-SPAIR, and axial T2WI-TSE-SPAIR. The diagnosis of ACL tear is mainly based on the sagittal T2WI-TSE-SPAIR sequence with the parameters: TE = 63 ms, TR = 2409 ms, FOV = 160 × 160 mm², matrix = 320 × 240, slice thickness = 3.5 mm, slice gap = 0.35 mm and 20 slices, Sensitivity Encoding (SENSE) factor = 2, number of signal averaged (NSA) = 1, and the scanning duration was 1 min and 40 s. The scanning sequences for the experimental group included coronal Proton Density Weighted Imaging-Turbo Spin Echo-Spectral Attenuated Inversion Recovery (PDWI-TSE-SPAIR) with the parameters TE = 30 ms, TR = 2351 ms, FOV = 130 × 130 mm², matrix = 260 × 214, slice thickness = 1.2 mm, slice gap = 0 mm and 15 slices, Sensitivity Encoding (SENSE) factor = 2, and number of signal averaged (NSA) = 5. The scanning duration was 2 min and 51 s. Analysis of imaging effect The imaging quality of the ACL in the experimental and control groups was rated on a 5-point scale [ 20 ]: 1 point, complete visualization in 0 layers (nondiagnostic); 2 points, complete visualization in 1 layer (poor); 3 points, complete visualization in 2 layers (adequate); 4 points, complete visualization in 3 layers (good); and 5 points, complete visualization in 4 or more layers (excellent). Scores of 4 or 5 were indicative of high-quality imaging. Patient details were anonymized in the picture archiving and communication system (PACS). Two physicians, A and B, with similar diagnostic experience, independently reviewed the images. Any disagreements were resolved by a senior physician, whose decision was final (this applied to both imaging results and diagnostic performance). Diagnostic performance The images were presented in a randomized sequence to gauge the inter-observer reliability. One week later, physician A reevaluated the randomized images to determine the intra-observer reliability. Diagnostic outcomes were categorized as either an ACL tear (both complete and partial tears) or no ACL tear. Arthroscopy is considered the “gold standard” for joint diagnosis. Its ability to directly visualize internal joint structures offers higher diagnostic accuracy than MRI or x-rays. Beyond its diagnostic capability, the minimally invasive nature of arthroscopy allows for immediate treatment, highlighting its precision and adaptability in joint assessments. Metrics such as diagnostic sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value (PPV), negative predictive value (NPV), and overall accuracy were derived for both study groups, using the arthroscopy findings as a benchmark. Statistical analysis Statistical analysis was performed with SPSS version 22.0 (IBM Corp, IL, USA) and MedCalc version 20.014 software (Mariakerke, Belgium). continuous data were presented as mean ± sd, and categorical data were presented as numbers (percentages). Reliability assessments were performed with Cohen’s kappa analysis [ 21 ]. Ordinal data comparison between the groups was performed with the Mann–Whitney U test. We derived diagnostic performance values with the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve, and the DeLong test ascertained the statistical variance between the area under curve (AUC) of the 2 groups. Results with a p value < 0.05 were considered statistically significant. The sample size calculation was performed using MedCalc version 20.014 software (Mariakerke, Belgium) and the calculation was based on the area under ROC curve(AUC) of diagnostic tests: assuming that the AUC of conventional extended-knee sagittal scans for diagnosing ACL tears was 0.7, it was expected that the new method (high-resolution oblique coronal scans based on the optimal flexed-knee angle) could improve the AUC to 0.9; setting significance (α) = 0.05, power(1-β) = 0.95, ratio of sample sizes in negative/positive groups = 1, it gave a total sample size of 60, with 30 negative cases and 30 positive cases required each. Results Study participants According to the set criteria, 92 patients- consisting of 25 women and 67 men aged 14–50 (average 29.2 ± 7.8 years) were selected. The average duration from injury to MRI examination was 4.2 ± 4.3 days. The primary activities causing injury were basketball (37.0%), non-sport falls/twists (19.6%), and soccer (16.3%). Patients’ clinical characteristics are presented in Table 1 . Table 1. Clinical characteristics of 92 knee joint injury patients Characteristic Total Age (year) 29.3 ± 7.8 Gender Women (%) 25 (27.2%) Men (%) 67 (72.8%) BMI (kg/m 2 ) 23.2 ± 3.4 Time from trauma to examination (day) 4.2 ± 4.3 Cause of trauma Basketball (%) 34 (36.9%) Fall sprain (not related to sports or vehicle accidents) (%) 18 (19.6%) Soccer (%) 15 (16.3%) Badminton (%) 10 (10.9%) Farm work (%) 8(8.7%) Running (%) 5(5.4%) Tennis (%) 2(2.2%) Open in a new tab BMI, body mass index. Inter-observer and intra-observer reliability analysis The inter-observer reliability was robust, with kappa values of 0.908 (95% CI: 0.820–0.996) for the experimental group and 0.825 (95% CI: 0.709–0.941) for the control group. Intra-observer reliability was similarly strong, with kappa values of 0.977 (95% CI: 0.932-1.000) and 0.956 (95% CI: 0.895-1.000) for the experimental and control groups, respectively. Observer assessments are presented in Table 2 . Table 2. Inter-observer and intra-observer reliability between the control and experimental group Observer A kappa p value + - Control group (SAG) Observer B + 46 5 0.825 (95%CI:0.709–0.941) <0.001 - 3 38 Observer A * + 48 1 0.956 (95% CI:0.895-1.000) <0.001 - 1 42 Experimental group (OCOR) Observer B + 55 3 0.908 (95%CI:0.820–0.996) <0.001 - 1 33 Observer A * + 56 1 0.977 (95% CI:0.932-1.000) <0.001 - 0 35 Open in a new tab SAG, sagittal; OCOR, oblique coronal; +, positive results; -, negative results. *: Physician A made the second evaluation one week later. Optimal flexion angle The ideal flexion angle for 50 volunteers was 19°-39°, averaging (30.3° ± 5.0°). In MRI oblique coronal scans with optimal flexion, the ACL had a nearly straight course with moderate tension, and both anterior-medial and posterior-lateral bundles were clearer in the same plane than that in the conventional extended-knee position. (Fig. 4 ). Fig. 4. Open in a new tab ACL visualization at various knee flexion angles: a1 Extended knee: MRI sagittal demonstrating ACL with higher tension and slight backward twist; a2 Corresponding oblique Coronal: ACL bundles appearing crossed due to twisting; b1 ~ 28° knee flexion: MRI sagittal depicting straight ACL with moderate tension; b2 Corresponding oblique Coronal: parallel and in-plane ACL bundles; c1 ~ 68° knee flexion: MRI sagittal revealing low-tensioned and forward-twisted ACL; c2 Corresponding oblique Coronal: twisted ACL bundles, reducing visibility. Red arrows indicate anterior-medial and posterior-lateral bundles of ACL. Imaging effect analysis The number of layers is determined according to the anatomical structure and course of ACL. When there are ≥ 3 layers of entire ACL display, it can be considered that ACL visualization is good. The entire ACL was visualized on ≥ 3 layers in 96.7% (89/92) of images in the experimental group compared with 12.0% (11/92) in the control group. Table 3 presents a detailed evaluation of these data, with significant differences between groups (Z = -11.266, p < 0.001). More importantly, our study noted that the images of the experimental group could show lesion features that could not be observed in the images of the control group, such as the tear of one of the branches of ACL and its degree of tear (Fig. 5 ). Table 3. Frequency counts for each level of images in the experimental and control group 1 point (0 slice) 2 points (1 slice) 3 points (2 slices) 4 points (3 slices) 5 points (≥ 4 slices) Z value p value Control group (SAG) 0 15 66 11 0 -11.266 <0.001 Experimental group (OCOR) 0 0 3 41 48 Open in a new tab Fig. 5. Open in a new tab Control group and experimental group images of a 41-year-old male patient with knee trauma. a-c Three consecutive layers with the best ACL visualization in traditional extended-knee sagittal images. The images show that there seems to be separation between the two branches of the ACL (white arrow), but it cannot be determined whether there is tearing of the branches; d-f Three consecutive layers with the best ACL visualization in high-resolution oblique coronal images at the optimal flexed-knee angle. The images clearly show complete tearing of the anterior medial bundle of ACL (red arrow) Diagnostic performance analysis With arthroscopy as a gold standard, the control group’s diagnostic measures for ACL tears were sensitivity = 76.3%, specificity = 81.8%, PPV = 88.2%, NPV = 65.9%, and accuracy = 78.3%. The experimental group had somewhat better values: sensitivity = 94.9%, specificity = 97.0%, PPV = 98.2%, NPV = 91.4%, and accuracy = 95.7% (Table 4 ). The ROC curve revealed higher AUC for the experimental group (0.945, 95% CI: 0.876–0.982) than for the control group (0.776, 95% CI: 0.678–0.857) ( p < 0.0001) (Fig. 6 ). Table 4. Comparison of diagnostic performance characteristics between the control group (SAG) and the experimental group (OCOR) for diagnosing ACL tears Control group (SAG) Experimental group (OCOR) p value + - + - Arthroscopy + 45 14 56 3 - 6 27 1 32 Sensitivity 76.3% 94.9% Specificity 81.8% 97.0% PPV 88.2% 98.2% NPV 65.9% 91.4% Accuracy 78.3% 95.7% AUC (95% CI) 0.776 (0.678–0.857) 0.945 (0.876–0.982) <0.0001 Open in a new tab SAG, sagittal; OCOR, oblique coronal; ACL, Anterior cruciate ligament; +, positive results; -, negative results; PPV, positive predictive value; NPV, negative predictive value; AUC, area under the curve. Fig. 6. Open in a new tab Comparison of ROC curve analysis between the control group (SAG) and the experimental group (OCOR) for diagnosing ACL tears. ROC, receiver operating characteristic Discussion The most important finding of the present study was that 30-degree knee flexion was the optimal angle for ACL visualization, and high-resolution oblique coronal imaging obtained at this specific angle could significantly enhance the visualization of the entire ACL and improve the diagnostic performance for ACL tears, particularly partial tears. Advances in imaging techniques have led to improvements in MRI performance in the evaluation of ACL tears, but challenges remain. Although the incorporation of MRI oblique coronal scans resulted in improved visualization and enhanced diagnostic capabilities [ 11 , 12 ], most of these investigations used an extended-knee position, which potentially overlooked the influence of ACL anatomy and knee flexion on the oblique coronal visualization. To address this gap, our study sought to ascertain an optimal plane in close alignment with the straight-line anatomy of the ACL, maintaining moderate tension. Such an alignment permitted high-resolution oblique coronal scans at specific knee flexion angles, which likely would enhance ACL tear diagnostic performance. Knees in 50 normal volunteers were first assessed with a knee joint dynamic scanning device; optimal knee flexion angle was found to range from 19° to 39°, averaging (30.3° ± 5.0°). Standardizing this value to 30° enabled consistent imaging among subjects, thus providing improved visibility and orientation of the ACL. The 30-degree knee flexion angle made the anatomical path of the 2 bundles of ACL straight and parallel, a preferred orientation for visualization during oblique coronal scanning and different from the “rotational twisting” often seen in conventional extended-knee position [ 11 , 22 , 23 ]. The 30º angle also was reasonably comfortable for trauma patients, even those with acute knee injuries, with the added benefit of short scan duration. Although our findings supported the validity of our imaging methodology, arthroscopic examination remains the gold standard for diagnosing cruciate ligament injuries [ 24 , 25 ]. However, in current medical practice, arthroscopy is not the first choice for diagnosing ACL tears. Crawford et al. [ 26 ] concluded that MRI is the most appropriate screening tool in diagnosing ACL tears before therapeutic arthroscopy, and it is preferable to diagnostic arthroscopy in most patients because it is faster and avoids surgical risks. In this study, the imaging of the ACL in its entirety was better in the experimental group than in the control group: In the 92 patients with knee trauma examined with our experimental imaging procedure, the sensitivity, specificity, PPV, NPV, and overall diagnostic accuracy were superior to those in the control group. Such results align with earlier research findings, supporting the higher efficacy of coronal-plane scans over traditional sagittal-plane imaging [ 12 , 27 , 28 ]. Nenezić and Kocijancic [ 29 ] reported that oblique sagittal imaging improved detection of ACL tear from 33 to 87%; Przeworski et al. [ 30 ] affirmed the value of oblique planes with 3D sequences in their MRI research on dogs; Richards et al. [ 31 ] pointed out that scanning additional volume sequences was beneficial for the diagnosis of complete ACL tears, but limited oblique T1 sequence of the intercondylar notch was unreliable; and Ng et al. [ 32 ] demonstrated the efficacy of oblique imaging in visualizing the ACL, with sensitivities up to 71%. However, most of these studies used extended-knee positioning. In the present study, the ROC curve analysis revealed superior optimal knee flexion angle imaging in the experimental group with an AUC of 0.945 compared with 0.776 for the control group. We feel that the results of our study may provide a basis for the preoperative assessment and surgical planning of ACL tears. Enhanced imaging can facilitate precise surgical procedures, faster recovery times, and improved surgical outcomes and reduce the risk of complications and rehabilitation costs. Despite its promising outcomes, our study has limitations. Being a single-center study, with a predominantly male sample from central China, the results lack generalizability. Also, regional differences, both anatomical and lifestyle or genetic factors, could influence ACL injury patterns and, consequently, the results of MRI imaging and surgery. Moreover, our study did not delve into gradations of ACL tear; such detailed observation might have provided further evidence of the diagnostic advantages of our technique. Finally, our study sample size, including volunteers and patients was not very large. Future research addressing these factors and others, such as varied demographics, larger sample size, various imaging coils, and leverage machine learning for refined image analysis deserve consideration. Conclusions High-resolution oblique coronal imaging at an optimal 30° flexed-knee angle offers enhanced visualization of the entire ACL and improved diagnostic accuracy for ACL tears compared with traditional extended-knee sagittal imaging. Clinicians could consider integrating a 30° flexed-knee oblique high-resolution scan into standard knee MRI evaluations of potential ACL injuries. Acknowledgements We thank Peng Wu (Clinical and Technical Support, Philips Healthcare, Shanghai 200072, China) for helping with scanning protocol optimization and valuable suggestions. Abbreviations MRI Magnetic resonance imaging ACL Anterior cruciate ligament ROC Receiver operating characteristic AUC Area under the curve T2WI T2 weighted imaging TSE Turbo spin echo SPAIR Spectral attenuated inversion recovery TE Echo time TR Repetition time FOV Field of view SENSE Sensitivity encoding NSA Number of signal averaged PDWI Proton density weighted imaging PACS Picture archiving and communication system PPV Positive predictive value NPV Negative predictive value Author contributions Conception and design of the research: CC, PZH and YJL; Optimization of equipment parameters: PW, YJL; Acquisition of image and data: CC, XL; Analysis and interpretation of the data: CC, XL; Statistical analysis: CC, QL; Funding acquisition: YJL; Writing of the manuscript: CC, XL and SP; Critical revision of the manuscript for intellectual content: YJL. All authors read and approved the final draft. Funding This research was funded by the Hunan Provincial Natural Science Foundation of China (grant number: S2020JJMSXM2267). Data availability No datasets were generated or analysed during the current study. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate This study adhered to the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki and was approved by the Institutional Review Board of Third Xiangya Hospital, Central South University (No:2019-S289). All participants provided signed informed consent forms. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Petersen W, Zantop T. Anatomy of the anterior cruciate ligament with regard to its two bundles. Clin Orthop Relat Res. 2007;454:35–47. 10.1097/BLO.0b013e31802b4a59. 10.1097/BLO.0b013e31802b4a59 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Śmigielski R, Zdanowicz U, Drwięga M, Ciszek B, Williams A. The anatomy of the anterior cruciate ligament and its relevance to the technique of reconstruction. Bone Joint J. 2016;81020–6. 10.1302/0301-620x.98b8.37117. 98-b. 10.1302/0301-620x.98b8.37117 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 3. Kaeding CC, Léger-St-Jean B, Magnussen RA. Epidemiology and diagnosis of Anterior Cruciate Ligament injuries. Clin Sports Med. 2017;36(1):1–8. 10.1016/j.csm.2016.08.001. 10.1016/j.csm.2016.08.001 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Takahashi S, Okuwaki T. Epidemiological survey of anterior cruciate ligament injury in Japanese junior high school and high school athletes: cross-sectional study. Res Sports Med. 2017;25(3):266–76. 10.1080/15438627.2017.1314290. 10.1080/15438627.2017.1314290 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Filardo G, Andriolo L, di Laura Frattura G, Napoli F, Zaffagnini S, Candrian C. Bone bruise in anterior cruciate ligament rupture entails a more severe joint damage affecting joint degenerative progression. Knee Surg Sports Traumatol Arthrosc. 2019;27(1):44–59. 10.1007/s00167-018-4993-4. 10.1007/s00167-018-4993-4 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Bordoni V, di Laura Frattura G, Previtali D, Tamborini S, Candrian C, Cristallo Lacalamita M, 