a Acute Care Surg. 2015;78:1122–1128. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Derderian SC, Greenan K, Mirsky DM, et al. The utility of magnetic resonance imaging in pediatric trauma patients suspected of having cervical spine injuries. J Trauma Acute Care Surg. 2019;87:1328–1335. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Franklin DB, III, Hardaway AT, Sheffer BW, et al. The role of computed tomography and magnetic resonance imaging in the diagnosis of pediatric thoracolumbar compression fractures. J Pediatr Orthop. 2019;39:e520–e523. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Drudi FM, Spaziani E, Di Filippo A, et al. Diagnosis and follow-up of minor cervical trauma. Clin Imaging. 2003;27:369–376. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Horal J, Nachemson A, Scheller S. Clinical and radiological long term follow-up of vertebral fractures in children. Acta Orthop Scand. 1972;43:491–503. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Angelliaume A, Simon A-L, Boissière L, et al. Conservative treatment of pediatric thoracic and lumbar spinal fractures: outcomes in the sagittal plane. J Pediatr Orthop B. 2017;26:73–79. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Moller A, Hasserius R, Besjakov J, et al. Vertebral fractures in late adolescence: a 27 to 47-year follow-up. Eur Spine J. 2006;15:1247–1254. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Singer G, Parzer S, Castellani C, et al. The influence of brace immobilization on the remodeling potential of thoracolumbar impaction fractures in children and adolescents. Eur Spine J. 2016;25:607–613. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Sirén A, Nyman M, Syvänen J, et al. Clinical outcome following magnetic resonance imaging as first-line imaging in low-impact pediatric spine trauma: a single-center retrospective observational study. Pediatr Radiol. 2023;53:2269–2280. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Zhang JF, Umenta J, Ali A, et al. Cervical spine flexion-extension radiograph